window.SUBJECTS.push({"id":"static","n":"Static GK","tab":"Static GK","w":"Dances, music, festivals, monuments, books, sports, awards and firsts.","topics":[{"id":"dance","n":"Dances","qs":[{"id":"q0040","q":"Guru Bipin Singh was a legend in the field of which classical dance?","o":["Kuchipudi","Odissi","Manipuri","Bharatnatyam"],"a":2,"x":"Guru Bipin Singh (1918–2000) is called the father of modern Manipuri dance; he received the Kalidas Samman.","e":"Manipuri's best-known forms: Ras Lila, and the Pung Cholom drum dance.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0063","q":"Mrinalini Sarabhai, a multifaceted artist, established which dance academy?","o":["Delhi Dance Academy","Darpana Academy of Performing Arts","Veve Dance Academy","House of Dance"],"a":1,"x":"Mrinalini Sarabhai founded the Darpana Academy of Performing Arts in Ahmedabad in 1949.","e":"She was a Bharatanatyam and Kathakali dancer; her daughter Mallika Sarabhai continues the academy.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0077","q":"Mallika Sarabhai is a noted exponent of which two Indian classical dance forms?","o":["Mohiniyattam and Sattriya","Kuchipudi and Bharatnatyam","Manipuri and Kathakali","Kathak and Odissi"],"a":1,"x":"Mallika Sarabhai is a noted exponent of Kuchipudi and Bharatanatyam and runs the Darpana Academy in Ahmedabad, founded by her mother Mrinalini Sarabhai.","e":"Kuchipudi comes from Andhra Pradesh; Bharatanatyam from Tamil Nadu.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0161","q":"Who among the following distinguished individuals has been bestowed with the Kalidas Samman, a prestigious award instituted by the Madhya Pradesh Government in 2006, because of exceptional contributions to Indian dance forms such as Kathak and Bharatanatyam in 2006, apart from many other awards like the Padma Bhushan (1992) and the Padma Vibhushan (2003)?","o":["Sonal Mansingh","Birju Maharaj","Mallika Sarabhai","Malavika Sarukkai"],"a":0,"x":"Sonal Mansingh, a leading Odissi and Bharatanatyam dancer, received the Padma Bhushan (1992), the Padma Vibhushan (2003) and Madhya Pradesh's Kalidas Samman.","e":"She was nominated to the Rajya Sabha in 2018.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"],"k":"The question says Kathak and Bharatanatyam; she is best known for Odissi and Bharatanatyam. DSSSB's answer, Sonal Mansingh, still stands."},{"id":"q0337","q":"Who among the following founded the Darpana Academy of Performing Arts in 1949?","o":["Mrinalini Sarabhai","Mallika Sarabhai","Rukmani Arundale","Birju Maharaj"],"a":0,"x":"Mrinalini Sarabhai, a Bharatanatyam and Kathakali dancer, founded the Darpana Academy of Performing Arts in Ahmedabad in 1949.","e":"Her daughter Mallika Sarabhai now leads Darpana.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0353","q":"The Kathak exponent who established the Kadamb School of Dance and Music in Ahmedabad and is known for her innovative choreography is:","o":["Shovana Narayan","Kumudini Lakhia","Maya Rao","Sitara Devi"],"a":1,"x":"Kumudini Lakhia, a Kathak innovator, founded the Kadamb Centre for Dance and Music in Ahmedabad in 1967.","e":"She received the Padma Vibhushan in 2025.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0362","q":"The two kings of Travancore, _________, structured the Mohiniyattam dance form of Kerala into its present classical form in the 18th–19th centuries.","o":["Anizham Tirunal and Uthrittathi Tirunal","Kartika Tirunal and Swati Tirunal","Moolam Tirunal and Chithira Tirunal","Avittom Tirunal and Vaisakha Tirunal"],"a":1,"x":"Mohiniyattam was given its present classical form by the Travancore kings Kartika Tirunal and Swathi Tirunal in the 18th–19th centuries; Vallathol revived it at Kerala Kalamandalam (1930).","e":"Mohiniyattam is a solo, graceful women's dance of Kerala in the lasya style.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0399","q":"Which classical dance form originated in the temples of Northern India and is characterised by intricate footwork and storytelling through mime?","o":["Bharatanatyam","Kathak","Kathakali","Kuchipudi"],"a":1,"x":"Kathak comes from north India's storytellers (kathakars), with fast footwork (tatkar), spins (chakkar) and mime.","e":"Gharanas: Lucknow, Jaipur, Banaras. Birju Maharaj belonged to the Lucknow gharana.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0403","q":"The famous classical dancer Kalamandalam Ramankutty Nair is associated with which dance form?","o":["Bharatanatyam","Odissi","Kathak","Kathakali"],"a":3,"x":"Kalamandalam Ramankutty Nair (1925–2013) was a great Kathakali actor, known for playing Hanuman and Ravana.","e":"Kathakali, from Kerala, uses elaborate make-up: green (pacha) for noble heroes.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0430","q":"To whom is the ancient Sanskrit text 'Natya Shastra' credited?","o":["Vyasa","Patanjali","Bharata Muni","Panini"],"a":2,"x":"The Natya Shastra, the ancient Sanskrit treatise on drama, dance and music, is credited to Bharata Muni.","e":"It sets out the nine rasas (navarasa) and the concept of bhava.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0465","q":"Kalamandalam Gopi is associated with which Indian classical dance form?","o":["Mohiniyattam","Kathakali","Odissi","Bharatanatyam"],"a":1,"x":"Kalamandalam Gopi is a leading Kathakali artist from Kerala, famous for 'pacha' (hero) roles such as Nala and Arjuna.","e":"He received the Padma Shri in 2009.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0478","q":"What is the term used to refer to the intricate and rhythmic footwork performed by Kathak dancers?","o":["Tatkar","Thattimetti","Mudras","Bhramari"],"a":0,"x":"Tatkar is the rhythmic footwork of Kathak, with ghungroos (bells) sounding the taal.","e":"Chakkar: the fast spins of Kathak.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1565","q":"Which of the following dances is NOT from eastern India?","o":["Bagh Nach","Ghumura","Kud dance","Chhau dance"],"a":2,"x":"Kud is a folk dance of Jammu and Kashmir, performed at night to honour Lok Devtas. Bagh Nach and Ghumura (Odisha) and Chhau (Odisha, Jharkhand, Bengal) are from eastern India.","e":"Chhau has three styles: Seraikela, Purulia and Mayurbhanj.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q1576","q":"Bakho-Bakhain and Panwariya are the dance forms of which state?","o":["Odisha","Bihar","Chhattisgarh","Uttarakhand"],"a":1,"x":"Bakho-Bakhain and Panwariya are folk dances of Bihar.","e":"Other Bihar dances: Jat-Jatin, Jhijhiya, Bidesia (folk theatre).","src":["Patwari 18 Sep 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q1583","q":"The Ministry of Culture, Government of India has included Chhau into the list of classical dances that makes a total of how many classical dance forms in India?","o":["8","7","10","9"],"a":3,"x":"The Ministry of Culture lists 9 classical dances, adding Chhau to the Sangeet Natak Akademi's 8: Bharatanatyam, Kathak, Kathakali, Kuchipudi, Odissi, Manipuri, Mohiniyattam and Sattriya.","e":"Sattriya (Assam) was the last added by the Akademi, in 2000.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S1"],"k":"Many books still say 8 (the Sangeet Natak Akademi list). This question's key follows the Ministry of Culture, which gives 9."},{"id":"q1621","q":"Which of the following Bhavas that are expressed in the classical dance are NOT matched properly to the meaning given in front of them?","o":["Shringar: Love","Adbhoot: Wonder","Karuna: Humorous","Bibhats: Disgust"],"a":2,"x":"Karuna means compassion or pathos, not humour (humour is hasya). The other pairs are right: shringara–love, adbhuta–wonder, bibhatsa–disgust.","e":"The nine rasas: shringara, hasya, karuna, raudra, veera, bhayanaka, bibhatsa, adbhuta, shanta.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q0484","q":"Which among the following dance forms of Gujarat was originally performed after the successful hunting of animals?","o":["Siddi Dhamal","Padhar","Dandiya","Tippani"],"a":0,"x":"Siddi Dhamal is performed by the Siddi community of Gujarat, of African origin, and was originally danced after a successful hunt.","e":"Gujarat's best-known folk dances: Garba and Dandiya Raas.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0524","q":"Dhol Cholom, a drum dance, is one of the dances performed during which of the following festivals in Manipur?","o":["Holi","Diwali","Navratri","Ganesh Chaturthi"],"a":0,"x":"Dhol Cholom, the drum dance of Manipur, is performed during Holi (Yaoshang) celebrations.","e":"Pung Cholom is the related mridanga (pung) dance.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0563","q":"The Ghumura dance form of Odisha is performed 15 days before the arrival of full moon in the month of ___________.","o":["October","September","May","June"],"a":1,"x":"Ghumura, the drum-dance of Kalahandi (western Odisha), is traditionally performed in the weeks before Dussehra, around September.","e":"The ghumura drum is an earthen pot covered with lizard skin.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0603","q":"Zemmado is an interesting dance form in which women belonging to the Dhangar community of Goa imitate the actions of which of the following animals?","o":["Elephant","Lion","Goat","Cow"],"a":2,"x":"Zemmado is a Goan folk dance of Dhangar women that imitates the movements of goats, the animals they herd.","e":"The Dhangars are a shepherd community of Goa and Maharashtra.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0643","q":"In which of the following dance forms of Rajasthan, women dancers carry earthen or brass pots which are set on fire?","o":["Chari","Kalbeliya","Ghoomar","Kachchhi"],"a":0,"x":"In the Chari dance of Rajasthan's Kishangarh (Gujjar community), women dance balancing brass pots (charis) on their heads, with lit cotton seeds dipped in oil in them.","e":"Other Rajasthan dances: Ghoomar (Bhil and Rajput), Kalbeliya (snake-charmer community), Kachchhi Ghodi.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0683","q":"Morulem is a traditional folk dance in Goa performed during which of the following festivals?","o":["Sunburn Festival","Shigmo Festival","Carnival","Lokrang Festival"],"a":1,"x":"Morulem is a folk dance of Goa performed during Shigmo, the spring festival of Goa.","e":"Other Goan dances: Dekhni, Fugdi, Kunbi.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0721","q":"Mrinalini Sarabhai was trained in which one of the following dance forms?","o":["Gotipua","Bihu","Bharatnatyam","Lavani"],"a":2,"x":"Mrinalini Sarabhai trained in Bharatanatyam (and Kathakali) and founded the Darpana Academy in Ahmedabad.","e":"Gotipua is from Odisha; Lavani from Maharashtra; Bihu from Assam.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1741","q":"Which of the following pair of State – dance is NOT correct? I. Gujarat - Tippani II. Assam - Domkach III. Rajasthan - Khoria","o":["Only II","I and II","II and III","Only III"],"a":2,"x":"Tippani is a Gujarati folk dance, so pair I is correct. Domkach belongs to Jharkhand and Bihar (not Assam) and Khoria to Haryana (not Rajasthan), so pairs II and III are wrong.","e":"Assam: Bihu; Rajasthan: Ghoomar, Kalbelia.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1783","q":"______ dance belongs to Mizoram.","o":["Baredi","Karma","Cheraw","Yakshagana"],"a":2,"x":"Cheraw, the bamboo dance, belongs to Mizoram; dancers step between clapping bamboo poles.","e":"Karma: Jharkhand/Chhattisgarh; Yakshagana: Karnataka.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1824","q":"______ dance belongs to Tripura.","o":["Santhal","Hozagiri","Karma","Fugdi"],"a":1,"x":"Hozagiri is a folk dance of the Reang (Bru) community of Tripura, performed balancing on pitchers.","e":"Other Tripura dances: Garia, Lebang.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1825","q":"Which of the following pair of State – dance is NOT correct? I. Andhra Pradesh – Kelika II.Haryana – Sua III. West Bengal – Chhau","o":["Only II","I and II","Only III","II and III"],"a":0,"x":"Sua is a Chhattisgarh dance, not Haryana's, so pair II is wrong; Chhau is performed in West Bengal (Purulia) and Kelika is listed among Andhra Pradesh's folk dances.","e":"Haryana dances: Saang, Khoria, Dhamal.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1976","q":"Kathakali dance belongs to the state of ______.","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Kerala","Maharashtra","Odisha"],"a":1,"x":"Kathakali is the classical dance-drama of Kerala, with elaborate make-up and costumes and stories from the epics.","e":"Kerala also has Mohiniyattam.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2021","q":"Sattriya dance originated in which state?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Bihar","Goa","Assam"],"a":3,"x":"Sattriya, founded by the saint Srimanta Sankardev in the 15th–16th century, originated in the sattras (monasteries) of Assam; it was recognised as a classical dance in 2000.","e":"India's eight classical dances include Sattriya.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2064","q":"Which of the following dance belongs to Goa?","o":["Tharu","Dekhni","Yakshagana","Chhau"],"a":1,"x":"Dekhni is a Goan folk dance, blending Indian and Western music, performed by women.","e":"Other Goa dances: Fugdi, Corredinho.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2066","q":"‘Swang’ dance belongs to which state?","o":["Odisha","Haryana","Andhra Pradesh","West Bengal"],"a":1,"x":"Swang (Saang) is a folk theatre and dance form of Haryana, telling stories through song and dialogue.","e":"Other Haryana forms: Khoria, Dhamal.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2101","q":"Which dance belongs to Jharkhand?","o":["Karaga","Santhal","Kullu Nati","Khoria"],"a":1,"x":"The Santhal dance belongs to Jharkhand (and Santhal areas of Bengal and Odisha). Karaga: Karnataka; Kullu Nati: Himachal Pradesh; Khoria: Haryana.","e":"Other Jharkhand dances: Jhumar, Paika, Chhau (Seraikela).","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2103","q":"Mohiniattam dance belongs to ______.","o":["Kerala","Andhra Pradesh","Assam","Tamil Nadu"],"a":0,"x":"Mohiniyattam, the graceful classical dance of the enchantress, belongs to Kerala.","e":"Kerala's two classical dances: Kathakali and Mohiniyattam.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2145","q":"Who out of the following is a leading exponent of Bharatanatyam and Kuchipudi dance and played the lead role of Draupadi in Peter Brook’s stage adaptation of the Mahabharata?","o":["Mallika Sarabhai","Ammu Swaminathan","Sonal Mansingh","Rukmini Devi Arundale"],"a":0,"x":"Mallika Sarabhai, a Bharatanatyam and Kuchipudi exponent, played Draupadi in Peter Brook's stage production of 'The Mahabharata' (1985).","e":"She is Mrinalini Sarabhai's daughter.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2390","q":"The name of Mohiniattam dance is derived from the word 'Mohini'. She is the female incarnation of which of the following gods?","o":["Lord Shiva","Lord Vishnu","Lord Brahma","Lord Indra"],"a":1,"x":"Mohiniyattam takes its name from Mohini, the enchantress form of Lord Vishnu.","e":"Kerala's 'dance of the enchantress'.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2480","q":"In which of the following places is the Tarangamel dance popular?","o":["Gujarat","Goa","Punjab","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Tarangamel is a folk dance of Goa, performed during Dussehra and Holi with colourful streamers.","e":"Other Goa dances: Dekhni, Fugdi.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2518","q":"Which of the following is the traditional dance of the Bodos?","o":["Nati","Raut Nacha","Oppanna","Bagurumba"],"a":3,"x":"Bagurumba (the 'butterfly dance') is the traditional dance of the Bodo people of Assam, performed at Bwisagu.","e":"Nati: Himachal; Raut Nacha: Chhattisgarh.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2520","q":"Which of the following dancers is a great exponent of Manipuri dance?","o":["Uday Shankar","Guru Bipin Singh","Sonal Mansingh","Padma Subrahmanyam"],"a":1,"x":"Guru Bipin Singh is the great exponent of Manipuri dance, credited with its revival and spread.","e":"Uday Shankar: modern dance; Sonal Mansingh: Odissi.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2660","q":"Which of the following pair of dance – part of India is correct? I. Kathak – Southern II. Kathakali – Northern","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Kathak is a North Indian classical dance and Kathakali a South Indian one (Kerala), so both pairs are wrong.","e":"Kathak: Uttar Pradesh; Kathakali: Kerala.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1297","q":"For which of the following dance-forms is Shovana Narayana very renowned?","o":["Bharatnatyam","Kathak","Lavani","Odissi"],"a":1,"x":"Shovana Narayan is a renowned Kathak dancer, awarded the Padma Shri in 1992.","e":"Other Kathak names: Birju Maharaj, Sitara Devi, Kumudini Lakhia.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1330","q":"Which among the following dance forms of Madhya Pradesh is also known as \"Harvest Dance\"?","o":["Bhagoria","Tertali","Matki","Jawara"],"a":3,"x":"Jawara is a harvest dance of Madhya Pradesh's Bundelkhand region, performed by men and women carrying baskets of jawara (sprouted grain).","e":"Other MP dances: Matki, Tertali, Bhagoria.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2701","q":"The Kuchipudi dance form was originated from which of the following states?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh","Maharashtra","Punjab"],"a":0,"x":"Kuchipudi originated in the village of Kuchipudi in Andhra Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2704","q":"Nongkrem dance is a traditional dance form celebrated by the people of ___________.","o":["Sikkim","Meghalaya","Tripura","Nagaland"],"a":1,"x":"Nongkrem is a Khasi harvest dance festival of Meghalaya, held at Smit near Shillong.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2746","q":"Dollu Kunitha is a traditional dance form famous in which of the following states?","o":["Gujarat","West Bengal","Karnataka","Maharashtra"],"a":2,"x":"Dollu Kunitha is a vigorous drum dance of Karnataka, performed by the Kuruba community.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2747","q":"Which among the following folk dance is not associated with Tamil Nadu?","o":["Chhau","Mayilattam","Therukoothu","Kummi"],"a":0,"x":"Chhau belongs to Odisha, Jharkhand and West Bengal, not Tamil Nadu. Mayilattam (peacock dance), Therukoothu (street theatre) and Kummi are Tamil Nadu folk forms.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2748","q":"Natya Shastra speaks of Eight Rasas. One of them is Bibhatsa. It translates to ________.","o":["Humorous","Heroism","Fear","Disgust"],"a":3,"x":"In the Natyashastra's eight rasas, Bibhatsa means disgust. The others: Shringara (love), Hasya (humour), Karuna (pathos), Raudra (anger), Veera (heroism), Bhayanaka (fear), Adbhuta (wonder).","e":"Shanta (peace) was added later as the ninth.","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0891","q":"Who among the following is a Padma Bhushan awardee associated with Kuchipudi dance?","o":["Rukmini Devi Arundale","Shovana Narayan","Sitara Devi","Vempati Chinna Satyam"],"a":3,"x":"Vempati Chinna Satyam, the Kuchipudi guru who founded the Kuchipudi Art Academy in Chennai, received the Padma Bhushan in 1998.","e":"Kuchipudi comes from Andhra Pradesh.","src":["PRT 16 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0978","q":"The Hurka Baul dance form belongs to which state?","o":["Punjab","Uttar Pradesh","West Bengal","Uttarakhand"],"a":3,"x":"Hurka Baul is a folk dance-song of Uttarakhand, performed in the fields during paddy and maize planting to the beat of the hurka drum.","e":"Other Uttarakhand dances: Choliya, Langvir Nritya, Barada Nati.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0993","q":"The 'Dumhal' dance is performed by which tribe in Jammu and Kashmir?","o":["Gujjar","Bhil","Wattal","Bakarwal"],"a":2,"x":"Dumhal is a dance of Jammu and Kashmir performed by men of the Wattal tribe, carrying a banner.","e":"Rouf is the women's dance of Kashmir.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1011","q":"Which classical dancer is credited with reviving Bharatanatyam and founding the Kalakshetra Foundation?","o":["Shovana Narayan","Alarmel Valli","Rukmini Devi Arundale","Mrinalini Sarabhai"],"a":2,"x":"Rukmini Devi Arundale revived Bharatanatyam and founded Kalakshetra in Chennai in 1936.","e":"She was also the first woman nominated to the Rajya Sabha (1952).","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1044","q":"Which classical dance form, originating from Kerala, is known for its elaborate facial expressions and hand gestures?","o":["Kathakali","Kuchipudi","Manipuri","Mohiniyattam"],"a":0,"x":"Kathakali of Kerala is known for its elaborate make-up, costumes, facial expressions (abhinaya) and hand gestures (mudras).","e":"It is traditionally performed by men, often all night.","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1051","q":"Which of the following is a popular folk dance form of Punjab performed during harvest festivals?","o":["Bhangra","Ghoomar","Lavani","Garba"],"a":0,"x":"Bhangra is Punjab's energetic harvest folk dance, performed around Baisakhi to the dhol.","e":"Giddha is the women's folk dance of Punjab.","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1119","q":"The semi-classical tribal dance style 'Chhau' is traditionally performed in which state of India?","o":["Kerala","Odisha","Gujarat","Punjab"],"a":1,"x":"Chhau is a semi-classical tribal martial dance of Odisha (Mayurbhanj), Jharkhand (Seraikela) and West Bengal (Purulia); UNESCO listed it in 2010.","e":"Purulia and Seraikela Chhau use masks; Mayurbhanj does not.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1120","q":"The famous classical dancer Mrinalini Sarabhai was associated with which two Indian classical dance forms?","o":["Kuchipudi and Kathakali","Bharatanatyam and Kathakali","Manipuri and Mohiniyattam","Kathak and Odissi"],"a":1,"x":"Mrinalini Sarabhai performed Bharatanatyam and Kathakali and founded Darpana Academy (Ahmedabad, 1949).","e":"Her daughter is Mallika Sarabhai.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1173","q":"The hand gestures used in classical Indian dances are known as:","o":["Bhavas","Mudras","Talas","Abhinayas"],"a":1,"x":"Mudras are symbolic hand gestures used in Indian classical dance to express meaning; the Natyashastra and Abhinaya Darpana describe them.","e":"Asamyukta hasta = one hand; samyukta hasta = both hands.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1248","q":"In which languages are the songs of Kuchipudi dance mainly composed?","o":["Sanskrit and Telugu","Tamil and Kannada","Malayalam and Marathi","Hindi"],"a":0,"x":"Kuchipudi songs are mainly in Telugu and Sanskrit; the dance comes from Kuchipudi village in Andhra Pradesh.","e":"Tarangam is the Kuchipudi item danced on the rim of a brass plate.","src":["PRT 3 Apr 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1259","q":"The tribal dance form “Karma” is traditionally performed by which community?","o":["Oraon","Apatani","Garo","Bhil"],"a":0,"x":"The Karma dance of the Karma festival is traditionally performed by the Oraon people (and other tribes such as the Munda) of Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and Odisha.","e":"It celebrates the Karam tree, a symbol of fortune.","src":["PRT 10 Apr 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1380","q":"The Garadi dance is a popular folk dance of which part of India?","o":["South India","West India","North India","North-east India"],"a":0,"x":"Garadi is a folk dance of Puducherry in south India, performed during festivals and linked with the legend of Rama's victory.","e":"Dancers wear bells and costumes resembling monkeys (vanaras).","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1395","q":"‘Buiya’ is a popular folk-dance form of which of the following states?","o":["Mizoram","Nagaland","Arunachal Pradesh","Manipur"],"a":2,"x":"Buiya is a folk dance of the Digaru Mishmi people of Arunachal Pradesh.","e":"Other Arunachal dances: Bardo Chham, Ponung, Aji Lamu.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1421","q":"Terms such as ‘Pada Bheda’, ‘Chalis’ and ‘Bhramaris’ are associated with the techniques of which of the following dance forms?","o":["Odissi","Kathakali","Ghoomar","Manipuri"],"a":0,"x":"Pada bheda (foot positions), chalis (gaits) and bhramaris (turns) are Odissi technique terms.","e":"Odissi's basic stances: tribhanga and chowk.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1430","q":"Which dance form was known as 'Dasi Attam' in earlier times?","o":["Mohiniattam","Bharatnatyam","Kathak","Bihu"],"a":1,"x":"Bharatanatyam was once called 'Dasi Attam' or 'Sadir', danced by devadasis in Tamil Nadu temples; E. Krishna Iyer and Rukmini Devi Arundale revived it.","e":"Rukmini Devi founded Kalakshetra in Chennai (1936).","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1514","q":"Which dancer is credited with integrating various traditions and techniques into their dance dramas?","o":["Uday Shankar","Sonal Mansingh","Birju Maharaj","Yamini Krishnamurthy"],"a":0,"x":"Uday Shankar, the pioneer of modern Indian dance, mixed classical, folk and Western stagecraft in his dance dramas; he set up the Almora cultural centre (1938).","e":"He was the elder brother of sitar maestro Ravi Shankar.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1521","q":"Who among the following Bharatnatyam and Kuchipudi dancers has been conferred with the Padma Shri, the Padma Bhushan and the Padma Vibhushan?","o":["Mrinalini Sarabhai","Yamini Krishnamurthy","Kalamandalam Kalyanikutty Amma","Shovana Narayan"],"a":1,"x":"Yamini Krishnamurthy, the Bharatanatyam and Kuchipudi exponent, received the Padma Shri (1968), Padma Bhushan (2001) and Padma Vibhushan (2016).","e":"She ran a dance institute in Delhi.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1542","q":"The dance form named after the mythical character Mohini belongs to which state?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Karnataka","Andhra Pradesh","Kerala"],"a":3,"x":"Mohiniyattam, named after Mohini (Vishnu's enchantress form), is the classical dance of Kerala.","e":"'Mohini' + 'attam' (dance) = the dance of the enchantress.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S3"]}]},{"id":"music","n":"Music and Instruments","qs":[{"id":"q0009","q":"The two distinct forms of Indian Classical Music, Hindustani and Carnatic, came into existence:","o":["after the advent of the French","after the advent of the British","after the advent of the Portuguese","after the advent of Muslims"],"a":3,"x":"Indian music split into the Hindustani (north) and Carnatic (south) traditions after the 13th century, when Persian and Central Asian influences reshaped northern music under the Delhi Sultanate.","e":"Amir Khusrau (13th–14th century) is credited with shaping early Hindustani forms such as qawwali.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0032","q":"Which of the following is NOT a general part of Indian classical music?","o":["Beats (Taal)","Rhythm (Laya)","Notes (Sur)","Devotion (Bhakti)"],"a":3,"x":"Classical music rests on swara or sur (notes), raga (melody), taal (rhythmic cycle) and laya (tempo). Devotion is a mood or theme, not a structural part.","e":"Teentaal, the most common Hindustani taal, has 16 beats.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0143","q":"Which of the following is a Hindustani classical vocal style known for its slow and meditative approach?","o":["Thumri","Tappa","Khayal","Dhrupad"],"a":3,"x":"Dhrupad is the oldest surviving Hindustani vocal style — slow, grave and meditative, and usually devotional.","e":"Masters: Tansen and the Dagar family. Khayal, thumri and tappa are later and lighter.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0192","q":"In Hindustani classical music, how can a 'raga' best be described?","o":["A form of Western notation","A rhythmic cycle","A type of musical instrument","A melodic framework for improvisation"],"a":3,"x":"A raga is a melodic framework — a set of notes with rules of ascent, descent and characteristic phrases — within which the performer improvises.","e":"A taal is the rhythmic cycle, not the raga.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0242","q":"The term ‘taal’ in Hindustani classical music refers to ___________.","o":["the rhythm or rhythmic cycle","the mood or emotion of the piece","the melody being performed","the tempo of the music"],"a":0,"x":"Taal is the rhythmic cycle of Indian classical music, a fixed number of beats (matras) grouped in sections.","e":"Teentaal: 16 beats; Jhaptaal: 10; Rupak: 7; Dadra: 6.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0244","q":"Ustad Hassu Khan and Ustad Haddu Khan are accredited to be the founders of the ______________________.","o":["Gwalior Gharana","Kirana Gharana","Banaras Gharana","Agra Gharana"],"a":0,"x":"Ustad Hassu Khan and Haddu Khan are regarded as founders of the Gwalior Gharana, the oldest khayal gharana.","e":"Kirana Gharana: Abdul Karim Khan. Agra: Faiyaz Khan. Jaipur-Atrauli: Alladiya Khan.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q1564","q":"Which of the following is a folk music of Bengal?","o":["Bhavageet","Baryi","Bihugeet","Baul"],"a":3,"x":"Baul is the mystic folk music of Bengal (West Bengal and Bangladesh), sung by wandering minstrels with the ektara. UNESCO lists it as intangible heritage.","e":"Bhavageete is from Karnataka; Bihugeet from Assam.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q1587","q":"Famous Sawai Gandharv Bhimsen Mahotsav music festival is organized in which of the following cities?","o":["Indore","Pune","Agra","Jaipur"],"a":1,"x":"The Sawai Gandharva Bhimsen Mahotsav, a major Hindustani classical music festival, is held in Pune every December; Pandit Bhimsen Joshi started it in 1953.","e":"Bhimsen Joshi (Kirana gharana) received the Bharat Ratna in 2009.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q0481","q":"Which among the following folk music of Madhya Pradesh is a heroic ballad song with intricate words?","o":["Alha","Pandavani","Pai","Dadra"],"a":0,"x":"Alha is a heroic ballad of Madhya Pradesh's Bundelkhand, telling of the warrior brothers Alha and Udal, and is sung in the rainy season.","e":"Pandavani (Chhattisgarh) retells the Mahabharata; Teejan Bai is its famous singer.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0522","q":"The Marfa music is associated with which of the following cities?","o":["Nagpur","Hyderabad","Kolkata","Bangalore"],"a":1,"x":"Marfa music is the rhythmic drum-and-daf music of the Hyderabadi Siddis (of African origin), played at weddings and celebrations in Hyderabad.","e":"It came with Siddi soldiers of the Nizams.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0564","q":"Borgeet is a Vaishnava devotional song, popular in which of the following states?","o":["Assam","Manipur","Meghalaya","Nagaland"],"a":0,"x":"Borgeet are Vaishnava devotional songs of Assam, composed by Srimanta Sankardeva and Madhavdeva in the 15th–16th centuries.","e":"Sankardeva also founded Sattriya dance and the satras (monasteries).","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0602","q":"The Pai songs are most popular folk songs in which of the following states?","o":["Maharashtra","Madhya Pradesh","Andhra Pradesh","West Bengal"],"a":1,"x":"Pai songs are sung in Madhya Pradesh, especially by Gond tribal people, at festivals.","e":"Other MP folk songs: Alha, Faag, Bhartrahari.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0644","q":"Which among the following folk music narrates the social and political conditions of Jammu and Kashmir?","o":["Jhoori","Surma","Chakri","Ladishah"],"a":3,"x":"Ladishah is a satirical folk ballad of Jammu and Kashmir: a wandering singer comments on social and political conditions, keeping rhythm with an iron rod (dhukar).","e":"Chakri is Kashmir's popular folk music, sung with the rabab and harmonium.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0684","q":"Ainchaliyan is a type of religious songs often sung to a newly wed bride or to an unmarried girl, who is expected to get married in near future. It is associated with which of the following states?","o":["Himachal Pradesh","Uttar Pradesh","Haryana","Punjab"],"a":0,"x":"Ainchaliyan are religious folk songs of Himachal Pradesh, sung for a newly married bride or a girl soon to be married.","e":"Other Himachal folk songs: Laman, Jhoori.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0722","q":"Amongst the following, Annapurna Devi was a notable performer of which musical instrument?","o":["Sarangi","Ghatam","Pakhawaj","Surbahar"],"a":3,"x":"Annapurna Devi, daughter of Ustad Allauddin Khan, was a great surbahar (bass sitar) player and guru of Hariprasad Chaurasia and Nikhil Banerjee.","e":"She was married to Pandit Ravi Shankar.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1739","q":"‘Jogi Sarangi’ is related to which categeory of musical instrument?","o":["Aerophone","Idiophone","Chordophone","Membranophone"],"a":2,"x":"The Jogi Sarangi is a bowed string instrument, so it is a chordophone; string instruments are chordophones.","e":"Chordophone = strings; aerophone = air; membranophone = skin; idiophone = body itself vibrates.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1781","q":"______ is a double reed wind instrument.","o":["Sundari","Khartal","Santoor","Surando"],"a":0,"x":"The Sundari is a double-reed wind instrument like a small shehnai, from Maharashtra.","e":"Khartal: wooden clappers; santoor: struck strings; surando: bowed strings.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1823","q":"Which of the following pair of State – Musical Instrument is NOT correct? I. Gujarat – Damaru II. West Bengal – Dhak","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"The dhak, a large drum, is closely linked with West Bengal's Durga Puja, so pair II is correct. The damaru is not a Gujarat instrument, so pair I is the wrong one.","e":"Dhak = Durga Puja drum.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1935","q":"Which of the following is NOT a Tata Vadya?","o":["Veena","Flute","Chitra","Santoor"],"a":1,"x":"Tata vadya are string instruments (veena, chitra veena, santoor); the flute is a wind instrument (sushira vadya).","e":"Natyashastra's four groups: tata (strings), sushira (wind), avanaddha (drums), ghana (solid).","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1977","q":"Which of the following pair of instrument – type is/are correct? I. Sushira Vadya – Nadaswaram II. Avanaddha Vadya – Damru","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The nadaswaram is a sushira vadya (wind instrument) and the damru an avanaddha vadya (membrane drum), so both pairs are correct.","e":"Sushira = wind; avanaddha = drums.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2102","q":"Which of the following is a stringed instrument? I. Santoor II. Gongs III. Violin","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","Only I"],"a":2,"x":"The santoor (struck strings) and the violin (bowed strings) are stringed instruments; gongs are percussion (idiophones).","e":"Gongs belong to the ghana vadya group.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2146","q":"By which name is the Indian classical singer Shivaputra Siddharamayya Komkalimath better known as?","o":["Pandit Lalgudi Jayaraman","Pandit Bhimsen Joshi","Pandit Jasraj","Pandit Kumar Gandharva"],"a":3,"x":"Shivaputra Siddharamayya Komkalimath is better known as Pandit Kumar Gandharva, a Hindustani vocalist known for Nirguni bhajans.","e":"He received the Padma Vibhushan (1990).","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2391","q":"Which of the following are examples of Reed instruments?","o":["Shehnai","Tabla","Ankya","Jhanj"],"a":0,"x":"The shehnai is a double-reed wind instrument, played at weddings and temples; the tabla is a drum and the jhanj cymbals.","e":"Ustad Bismillah Khan made the shehnai famous.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2435","q":"Which of the following vocalists of India had penned many compositions with the name \"Ram Rang\"?","o":["Shubha Mudgal","Pandit Jasraj","Pandit M. Balamuralikrishna","Pandit Ramashreya Jha"],"a":3,"x":"Pandit Ramashreya Jha, a Hindustani vocalist and composer, wrote many bandishes under the pen name 'Ram Rang'.","e":"A bandish is a fixed composition in a raga.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2477","q":"Which of the following is Kaushiki Chakraborty associated with?","o":["Sitar","Sarod","Tabla","Vocal"],"a":3,"x":"Kaushiki Chakraborty is a Hindustani classical vocalist of the Patiala gharana, daughter of Pandit Ajoy Chakrabarty.","e":"Vocal, not an instrument.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2519","q":"Which language is Rabindra Sangeet composed in?","o":["Odiya","Hindi","Bengali","Assamese"],"a":2,"x":"Rabindra Sangeet, the songs of Rabindranath Tagore (over 2,000), is in Bengali.","e":"Tagore wrote the national anthems of India and Bangladesh.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2589","q":"Which of the following is/are a tabla player? I. Zakir Hussian II. Shafat Ahmed","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Zakir Hussain and Shafaat Ahmed Khan were both tabla players.","e":"Zakir Hussain was the son of tabla maestro Ustad Alla Rakha.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2661","q":"Which of the following are solid instruments which do not require tuning?","o":["Tata Vadya","Ghana Vadya","Avanaddha Vadya","Sushira Vadya"],"a":1,"x":"Ghana vadya are solid instruments such as cymbals, bells and ghatam that are struck and need no tuning.","e":"Tata strings, sushira wind, avanaddha drums, ghana solid.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1331","q":"Which among the following folk music evolved among the Goan Catholics of Goa?","o":["Maand","Powada","Bhavageete","Mando"],"a":3,"x":"Mando is a folk song-dance form of Goa's Catholics, blending Indian and Portuguese musical traditions.","e":"Dulpod is the faster Goan song that often follows a Mando.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2702","q":"Kajri folk music is associated with which of the following states?","o":["Kerala","Karnataka","Madhya Pradesh","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"Kajri is a folk song form of Uttar Pradesh (Mirzapur, Varanasi), sung in the rainy season.","e":"Kajri = monsoon songs.","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0949","q":"Jhuri is the folk music of which state?","o":["Bihar","Gujarat","Rajasthan","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"Jhuri is a folk song form of Himachal Pradesh, sung about romance and longing.","e":"Other Himachali folk forms: Nati (dance), Laman, Samskara songs.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q0971","q":"Which of the following is a major tradition of Indian classical music that originated in North India?","o":["Folk","Hindustani","Carnatic","Modern"],"a":1,"x":"Hindustani music developed in North India; Carnatic music is the South Indian classical tradition.","e":"Hindustani forms: dhrupad, khayal, thumri; Carnatic: kriti, varnam.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1190","q":"The 'Pallavi' section in Carnatic music is best described as:","o":["A single thematic line elaborated upon","A detailed depiction of mythological stories","The concluding devotional song","The fastest part of improvisation"],"a":0,"x":"In a Carnatic kriti the Pallavi is the opening thematic line, repeated and elaborated; it is followed by the Anupallavi and Charanam.","e":"Pallavi → Anupallavi → Charanam.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1207","q":"In which classical music style are instruments like the veena, mridangam and violin mainly used in performance?","o":["Hindustani","Khayal","Carnatic","Dhrupad"],"a":2,"x":"Carnatic music performances typically use the veena, mridangam and violin (with ghatam and kanjira).","e":"Hindustani uses tabla, sitar, sarangi and harmonium.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1384","q":"Which of the following pairs of personality and the associated musical instrument is NOT matched correctly?","o":["Zakir Hussain - Tabla","Bismillah Khan -Shehnai","Bhajan Sopori - Veena","Hari Prasad Chaurasia - Flute"],"a":2,"x":"Bhajan Sopori was a santoor player from Kashmir, not a veena player. The other pairs are correct.","e":"Other santoor maestro: Shivkumar Sharma.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1417","q":"Who among the following is NOT amongst the Trinity of Carnatic music?","o":["Tyagaraja","Syama Sastri","Tenali Raman","Muthuswami Dikshitar"],"a":2,"x":"The Trinity of Carnatic music is Tyagaraja, Muthuswami Dikshitar and Syama Sastri, all of the 18th–19th centuries. Tenali Raman was a poet at Krishnadevaraya's court.","e":"Purandara Dasa is called the 'father of Carnatic music'.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1432","q":"Ustad Zakir Hussain, the Indian music maestro, is known for playing which instrument?","o":["Flute","Tabla","Santoor","Harmonica"],"a":1,"x":"Ustad Zakir Hussain was a tabla maestro, son of Ustad Alla Rakha, and a Padma Vibhushan and Grammy winner.","e":"Other tabla greats: Alla Rakha, Kishan Maharaj.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1551","q":"To propagate Indian Dance and Music, the erstwhile government of united Andhra Pradesh started Sri Thyagaraja Government college of Music and Dance in the year 1952 at _________.","o":["Hyderabad","Vizianagaram","Kakinada","Visakhapatnam"],"a":0,"x":"Sri Thyagaraja Government College of Music and Dance was set up in Hyderabad in 1952 to promote Indian music and dance.","e":"It is named after the Carnatic composer Tyagaraja.","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]}]},{"id":"festivals","n":"Fairs and Festivals","qs":[{"id":"q1568","q":"Which of the following is celebrated in Assam to pray for the standing crops?","o":["Kati Bihu","Rongali Bihu","Bohag Bihu","Magh Bihu"],"a":0,"x":"Kati (Kongali) Bihu in October–November is when Assamese farmers light earthen lamps in the paddy fields to pray for the standing crop.","e":"The three Bihus: Rongali/Bohag (April, new year), Kati/Kongali (October), Bhogali/Magh (January, harvest).","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q1585","q":"In which state is the festival of Bastar Dussehra celebrated?","o":["Bihar","Maharashtra","Chhattisgarh","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"Bastar Dussehra in Jagdalpur, Chhattisgarh, runs for about 75 days and honours goddess Danteshwari, not Rama.","e":"It is among the longest festivals in the world.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q1617","q":"The Chapcharkut Festival is celebrated in which of the following state?","o":["Sikkim","Mizoram","Tripura","Assam"],"a":1,"x":"Chapchar Kut, Mizoram's spring festival, is held in March after jungle-clearing for jhum (shifting) cultivation.","e":"Other Mizo festivals: Mim Kut and Pawl Kut (harvest).","src":["Patwari 18 Sep 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q0483","q":"The Chhari Festival is celebrated in which of the following states/union territories of India?","o":["Odisha","Haryana","Karnataka","Jammu and Kashmir"],"a":3,"x":"The Chhari festival belongs to Jammu and Kashmir: the holy mace (Chhari Mubarak) of Lord Shiva is carried to the Amarnath cave.","e":"The Amarnath Yatra ends on Shravan Purnima (Raksha Bandhan).","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0523","q":"Karma is a harvest festival which is held on the 11th day of a Purnima of the Hindu month of ___________.","o":["Chaith","Kartik","Phagun","Bhado"],"a":3,"x":"Karma, the tribal harvest festival of Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and Odisha, is held on the 11th day of the bright fortnight (Purnima) of Bhado (Bhadrapada).","e":"People dance around a branch of the Karam tree.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0562","q":"The Gochi Festival is associated with which of the following states?","o":["Assam","Haryana","Himachal Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"Gochi is a festival of the Lahaul valley in Himachal Pradesh, celebrated in February for the birth of sons in the past year.","e":"Other Himachal fairs: Kullu Dussehra, Minjar (Chamba), Lavi (Rampur).","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0604","q":"Kharchi Puja is a festival which involves worshipping of ____________ gods who forms the dynasty deity of the Tripuri people.","o":["11","12","13","14"],"a":3,"x":"Kharchi Puja in Agartala, Tripura (July), worships 14 gods (Chaturdasha Devata), the family deities of the Tripuri royal line.","e":"It is a week-long festival.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0642","q":"The Bhadu Festival celebrates the transformation of Princess Bhadreswari into a cult figure. It is celebrated in which of the following states?","o":["Karnataka","West Bengal","Kerala","Maharashtra"],"a":1,"x":"Bhadu is a harvest-season festival of West Bengal's Purulia, Bankura and Birbhum districts in the month of Bhadra, honouring Princess Bhadreswari of Kashipur, who became a folk goddess.","e":"Tusu is another Bengal–Jharkhand folk festival, held in Paush (January).","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0682","q":"Dhanuyatra is a great mass festival of Odisha which is based on the noble deeds of Lord ____________.","o":["Ram","Ganesh","Krishna","Hanuman"],"a":2,"x":"Dhanu Yatra, held in Bargarh, Odisha, re-enacts Lord Krishna's story up to Kansa's death; the whole town becomes the stage.","e":"It is described as the world's largest open-air theatre.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1737","q":"Which of the festival is associated with Bihar?","o":["Chumpha","Khasi","Bonalu","Madhushravani"],"a":3,"x":"Madhushravani is a festival of newly married women in the Mithila region of Bihar, held in the month of Sawan (July–August).","e":"Chumpha: Manipur; Bonalu: Telangana.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1780","q":"Chad Sukra (sowing festival) festival is associated with which state?","o":["Karnataka","Haryana","Meghalaya","Assam"],"a":2,"x":"Chad Sukra is a sowing festival of the Pnar (Jaintia) people of Meghalaya, held in April–May.","e":"Other Meghalaya festivals: Nongkrem, Wangala, Behdienkhlam.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1821","q":"Pongal festival is associated with which state?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Tamil Nadu","Assam","Mizoram"],"a":1,"x":"Pongal is Tamil Nadu's four-day harvest festival in mid-January, marking the sun's movement north (Makar Sankranti).","e":"Bhogi, Thai Pongal, Mattu Pongal, Kaanum Pongal.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1937","q":"Halda festival belongs to which state?","o":["Himachal Pradesh","Haryana","Rajasthan","Odisha"],"a":0,"x":"Halda is a winter festival of the Lahaul valley in Himachal Pradesh, dedicated to the goddess of wealth, Shiskar Apa.","e":"Other Himachal festivals: Kullu Dussehra, Minjar, Lavi.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1978","q":"Which of the following festival belongs to Uttrakhand? I. Khatarua II. Phool Dei","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Khatarua (an autumn fire festival) and Phool Dei (a spring flower festival) are both festivals of Uttarakhand.","e":"Other Uttarakhand fairs: Kumbh at Haridwar, Nanda Devi Raj Jat.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2023","q":"Which of the following festival belongs to Assam? I. Me-Dum-Me-Phi II. Baishagu","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Me-Dam-Me-Phi (the Ahom ancestor-worship festival, 31 January) and Baishagu (the Bodo spring festival) are both celebrated in Assam.","e":"Also Bihu in Assam: Rongali, Kongali, Bhogali.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2063","q":"Adiperukku festival is associated with which state?","o":["Karnataka","Tamil Nadu","Telangana","Assam"],"a":1,"x":"Aadi Perukku is a Tamil Nadu festival honouring water and rivers, held in the Tamil month of Aadi (July–August).","e":"It is especially celebrated along the Kaveri.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2104","q":"Which of the following pair of state – festival is NOT correct? I.Bihar – Baishagu II. Kerala – Onam","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"Baishagu is the spring festival of the Bodo people of Assam, not Bihar, so pair I is wrong; Onam belongs to Kerala.","e":"Bihar: Chhath, Sama-Chakeva.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2143","q":"In which of the following Indian months is the festival Rakshabandhan celebrated?","o":["Sravana","Bhadra","Ankita","Chaitra"],"a":0,"x":"Raksha Bandhan is celebrated on the full moon (Purnima) of the Hindu month of Shravana (July–August).","e":"Shravana Purnima also marks Narali Purnima in Maharashtra.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2388","q":"Why Pongal is celebrated?","o":["To celebrate the harvest of rice, sugarcane etc","To celebrate the victory of Shiva over asurs","To thanks Lord Shiva for the creation of the world","To mark the birthday of Guru Chaitanya"],"a":0,"x":"Pongal is Tamil Nadu's harvest festival, thanking the Sun god for the harvest of rice, sugarcane and turmeric.","e":"'Pongal' is also the sweet rice dish cooked that day.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2433","q":"For which of the following states is the festival Puthandu celebrated as the New Year?","o":["Kerala","Tamil Nadu","Andhra Pradesh","Karnataka"],"a":1,"x":"Puthandu, the Tamil New Year, falls on 14 April, the first day of the Tamil month Chithirai.","e":"Vishu is Kerala's New Year on the same day.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2434","q":"In which of the following states is the Modhera Film Festival celebrated?","o":["Maharashtra","Gujarat","Madhya Pradesh","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"The Modhera festival (Uttarardh Mahotsav) is held at the Modhera Sun Temple in Gujarat each January.","e":"Modhera Sun Temple: Solanki king Bhima I, 1026.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"],"k":"The event is a classical dance festival, not a film festival as the question says."},{"id":"q2479","q":"In which of the following Indian states is the Bathukamma festival celebrated?","o":["Telangana","Tamil Nadu","Karnataka","Kerala"],"a":0,"x":"Bathukamma is Telangana's floral festival, celebrated by women during Navratri with flowers stacked like a temple tower.","e":"Telangana's other festival: Bonalu.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2590","q":"Which of the following festival belongs to Kerala? I. Onam II. Vishu","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Onam (harvest festival, King Mahabali) and Vishu (New Year) are both Kerala festivals.","e":"Onam: Chingam month (Aug–Sep); Vishu: April.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2659","q":"Gogaji fair belongs to ______.","o":["Rajasthan","Punjab","Andhra Pradesh","Kerala"],"a":0,"x":"The Gogaji fair is held at Gogamedi in Hanumangarh, Rajasthan, honouring the folk deity Goga Veer (the snake-god).","e":"Held in Bhadrapada (Aug–Sep).","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1300","q":"In which of the following states is the Bihula festival very popular?","o":["Bihar","Madhya Pradesh","Odisha","Gujarat"],"a":0,"x":"Bihula (Bishahari) puja, honouring the snake goddess Manasa and the devoted Behula, is popular in the Bhagalpur region of Bihar.","e":"Manjusha art of Bhagalpur illustrates the Bihula–Bishahari story.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2703","q":"The Hemis Festival is a colourful ________-day affair that falls on the 10th day of the Tibetan Lunar month.","o":["four","one","two","three"],"a":2,"x":"The Hemis Festival in Ladakh is a two-day festival on the 10th day of the Tibetan lunar month, marking Guru Padmasambhava's birth, with masked Cham dances.","e":"Held at Hemis monastery.","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2749","q":"Saga Dawa is one of the major festivals associated with which of the following states?","o":["Meghalaya","Sikkim","Tripura","Nagaland"],"a":1,"x":"Saga Dawa, marking the Buddha's birth, enlightenment and death, is a major Buddhist festival in Sikkim.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1470","q":"The Nalwari Fair is a cattle fair celebrated in which state?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Himachal Pradesh","Assam","Gujarat"],"a":1,"x":"The Nalwari Fair is a cattle fair held in March at Bilaspur, Himachal Pradesh.","e":"Other Himachal fairs: Minjar (Chamba), Lavi (Rampur), Shivratri (Mandi).","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q0782","q":"The Ugadi festival is celebrated in which of the following states of India?","o":["Assam","Odisha","Madhya Pradesh","Karnataka"],"a":3,"x":"Ugadi is the New Year festival of Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, held on the first day of Chaitra.","e":"Maharashtra celebrates the same day as Gudi Padwa.","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2025 S1"]}]},{"id":"art","n":"Monuments, Art and Architecture","qs":[{"id":"q0001","q":"In which of the following States of India is the Bolgatty Palace located?","o":["Punjab","Goa","Karnataka","Kerala"],"a":3,"x":"Bolgatty Palace stands on Bolgatty (Mulavukad) Island off Kochi, Kerala. The Dutch built it in 1744; it later became the home of the British Resident.","e":"Kochi's two palace names to keep apart: Bolgatty Palace and Mattancherry (the Dutch Palace).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0069","q":"The Bagh Caves are considered important examples of which architectural tradition?","o":["Islamic architecture","Hindu architecture","Jain architecture","Buddhist architecture"],"a":3,"x":"The Bagh Caves (Dhar district, Madhya Pradesh) are nine rock-cut Buddhist monasteries from the Gupta period, famous for murals similar to Ajanta's.","e":"Buddhist rock-cut sites: Ajanta, Bagh, Karla, Bhaja, Kanheri. Ellora mixes Buddhist, Hindu and Jain caves.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0092","q":"Which of the following is a prominent example of British colonial architecture in India?","o":["Konark Temple","Hawa Mahal","Victoria Memorial","Sun Temple"],"a":2,"x":"The Victoria Memorial in Kolkata (1906–1921), in white Makrana marble, is a leading example of British colonial (Indo-Saracenic) architecture.","e":"Other colonial landmarks: Gateway of India (Mumbai), Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus, Rashtrapati Bhavan (Lutyens).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0112","q":"The Sanchi Stupa has how many profusely carved ornamental gateways?","o":["Four","Two","Three","Six"],"a":0,"x":"The Great Stupa at Sanchi has four elaborately carved toranas (gateways), one at each cardinal direction, added in the 1st century BCE.","e":"Ashoka commissioned the original brick stupa in the 3rd century BCE.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0125","q":"In which of the following centuries was the Jagannath temple built?","o":["9th century AD","12th century AD","11th century AD","13th century AD"],"a":1,"x":"The present Jagannath Temple at Puri was built in the 12th century by Anantavarman Chodaganga Deva of the Eastern Ganga dynasty.","e":"Puri is one of the four Char Dham; its Rath Yatra takes place in Ashadha (June–July).","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0146","q":"Which temple is located at ‘Kuber Tila’ of Ayodhya?","o":["Durga Temple","Sun Temple","Shiv Temple","Krishna Temple"],"a":2,"x":"Kuber Tila in Ayodhya has an ancient Shiva temple; a statue of Jatayu was also installed there in 2024.","e":"Kuber Tila lies within the Ram Janmabhoomi temple complex.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0149","q":"The Brihadeeswara Temple in Thanjavur is primarily dedicated to which Hindu deity?","o":["Lord Vishnu","Lord Ganesha","Lord Shiva","Goddess Durga"],"a":2,"x":"The Brihadeeswara Temple at Thanjavur, built by Rajaraja Chola I (completed 1010 CE), is dedicated to Lord Shiva.","e":"It is part of the UNESCO 'Great Living Chola Temples' with Gangaikondacholapuram and Darasuram.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0150","q":"Which of the following temples is known for its grand architecture, including a massive statue of Nandi and the tallest tower in the world?","o":["Meenakshi Temple, Madurai","Somnath Temple, Gujarat","Brihadeeswara Temple, Thanjavur","Virupaksha Temple, Hampi"],"a":2,"x":"The Brihadeeswara Temple at Thanjavur has a huge monolithic Nandi and a vimana about 66 m tall, among the tallest temple towers of its age.","e":"The capstone at the top of the vimana is said to weigh about 25 tonnes.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0159","q":"Which landmark is a major example of colonial architecture in Kolkata?","o":["Basilica of Bom Jesus","National Gallery of Modern Art","Gateway of India","Victoria Memorial"],"a":3,"x":"The Victoria Memorial in Kolkata is the city's best-known colonial monument, designed by William Emerson.","e":"Basilica of Bom Jesus is in Goa; Gateway of India is in Mumbai.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0168","q":"Which of the following is correctly matched with respect to the location of monuments?","o":["Mehrangarh Fort - Amritsar","Hawa Mahal - Jabalpur","Mecca Masjid - Hyderabad","Golconda Fort - Bijapur"],"a":2,"x":"Mecca Masjid is in Hyderabad, begun under Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah in 1617. Mehrangarh is in Jodhpur, Hawa Mahal in Jaipur, and Golconda Fort in Hyderabad.","e":"Gol Gumbaz is the famous monument of Bijapur.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0201","q":"In which of the following states of India, Kunjaban Palace, also known as‘Pushpabanta Palace’, is located?","o":["Bihar","Rajasthan","Meghalaya","Tripura"],"a":3,"x":"Kunjaban Palace, also called Pushpabanta Palace, is in Agartala, Tripura, built by Maharaja Birendra Kishore Manikya in 1917. It is now the official residence of the Governor.","e":"Agartala's other palace, Ujjayanta Palace, now houses the state museum.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0207","q":"The lofty and impressive gates of South Indian styles of temples are known as ______.","o":["vimanas","mandapas","gopurams","shikharas"],"a":2,"x":"Gopurams are the tall, carved gateway towers of South Indian (Dravida) temples.","e":"The tower over the sanctum is the vimana in the south and the shikhara in the north.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0212","q":"The Namdroling Monastery, popularly referred to as‘The Golden Temple ’, is one of the largest Tibetan settlements in India located in the state of _____.","o":["Karnataka","Goa","Bihar","Maharashtra"],"a":0,"x":"Namdroling Monastery, the 'Golden Temple' at Bylakuppe near Kushalnagar in Karnataka, is a major Nyingma monastery in one of the largest Tibetan settlements in India.","e":"Tawang Monastery in Arunachal Pradesh is India's largest monastery.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0225","q":"The Sun Temple at Konark is considered an invaluable link in the history of the diffusion of the cult of Surya. Which region is believed to have been the origin of this cult in the 8th Century?","o":["Kashmir","Tamil Nadu","Rajasthan","Maharashtra"],"a":0,"x":"UNESCO describes Konark as a link in the spread of the Surya cult, which began in Kashmir in the 8th century and reached eastern India.","e":"The Konark Sun Temple (13th century, Narasimhadeva I of the Eastern Gangas) is built as a chariot with 24 wheels.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0235","q":"In which of the following years, the Chapora Fort of Goa was built by the Portuguese?","o":["1617","1607","1727","1787"],"a":0,"x":"The present Chapora Fort in North Goa was built by the Portuguese in 1617 on the site of an earlier Adil Shahi fort.","e":"It became famous as the 'Dil Chahta Hai fort'.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0292","q":"Which monastery belongs to the Nyingma sect of Tibetan Buddhism founded in 1701 CE?","o":["Dubdi Monastery","Kardang Monastery","Likir Monastery","Gandhola Monastery"],"a":0,"x":"Dubdi Monastery at Yuksom, Sikkim, founded in 1701, belongs to the Nyingma sect and is considered Sikkim's oldest monastery.","e":"Yuksom was Sikkim's first capital (1642).","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0299","q":"Naggar Castle, a 15th-century historic fort-palace, is located in which of the following states of India?","o":["Bihar","Andhra Pradesh","Tripura","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"Naggar Castle, a 15th-century stone and timber fort-palace in the Kullu valley, is in Himachal Pradesh.","e":"Nearby is the Nicholas Roerich Art Gallery.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0311","q":"In which year was the foundation stone of the All India War Memorial Arch in Delhi, now known as India Gate, laid?","o":["1921","1931","1926","1939"],"a":0,"x":"The foundation stone of the All India War Memorial (India Gate) in Delhi was laid in 1921 by the Duke of Connaught. Edwin Lutyens designed it and it was finished in 1931.","e":"It honours about 70,000 Indian soldiers killed in World War I.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0313","q":"What is the shape of the main temple tower, known as the vimana, in Dravidian temples?","o":["Curved spire","Dome-like structure","Square","Stepped pyramid"],"a":3,"x":"In Dravida temples the vimana over the sanctum rises as a stepped pyramid; in Nagara (north Indian) temples the shikhara is curvilinear.","e":"Example: the vimana of the Brihadeeswara Temple at Thanjavur.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0316","q":"Most of the main temple sites of ancient Kalinga in Odisha are located in which modern district?","o":["Bhubaneswar district","Konark district","Cuttack district","Puri district"],"a":3,"x":"Most major temple sites of ancient Kalinga are in today's Puri district: the Jagannath Temple and the Konark Sun Temple.","e":"The Lingaraj Temple is in Bhubaneswar (Khordha district).","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0333","q":"_________ is associated with the architectural design of the Rashtrapati Bhavan during the period of the British Raj.","o":["Sir Edwin Lutyens","Sir Robert Chisholm","Sir Laurie Baker","Sir Henry Irwin"],"a":0,"x":"Sir Edwin Lutyens designed Rashtrapati Bhavan (formerly Viceroy's House, completed 1929) for the new capital of New Delhi; Herbert Baker designed the North and South Blocks.","e":"Lutyens also designed India Gate.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0363","q":"The 'Se Cathedral' church of Goa was built in the _________ architectural style in the 16th Century.","o":["Italianate","Byzantine","Romanesque","Manueline"],"a":3,"x":"Se Cathedral in Old Goa, built by the Portuguese from 1562, is in Portuguese-Manueline style outside with a Corinthian interior; it is dedicated to St Catherine.","e":"Nearby, the Basilica of Bom Jesus holds the relics of St Francis Xavier.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0391","q":"The Hawa Mahal is located in which Indian city?","o":["Delhi","Jaisalmer","Jaipur","Agra"],"a":2,"x":"Hawa Mahal ('Palace of Winds', 1799) in Jaipur was built by Maharaja Sawai Pratap Singh, with 953 small windows (jharokhas) so royal women could watch the street.","e":"Jaipur is the 'Pink City'; its walled city is a UNESCO World Heritage site.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0431","q":"Who is credited with the construction of the Great Stupa at Sanchi?","o":["Kanishka","Chandragupta Maurya","Ashoka","Harsha"],"a":2,"x":"Emperor Ashoka commissioned the Great Stupa at Sanchi (3rd century BCE) as a brick structure; the Shungas later enlarged it in stone and the Satavahanas added gateways.","e":"Sanchi is in Raisen district, Madhya Pradesh; it is a UNESCO site.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0440","q":"In which state of India is the Sun Temple of Konark located?","o":["Bihar","Odisha","Maharashtra","Karnataka"],"a":1,"x":"The Konark Sun Temple (13th century, Narasimhadeva I of the Eastern Ganga dynasty) is in Puri district, Odisha, and is called the 'Black Pagoda'.","e":"It is shown on the back of the ₹10 note.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0471","q":"Which Buddhist architectural structure is entirely made of brick and still stands, even in the 21st century?","o":["Great Stupa of Sanchi","Mahabodhi Temple","Amaravati Stupa","Dhamek Stupa"],"a":1,"x":"The Mahabodhi Temple at Bodh Gaya is among the earliest Buddhist temples built wholly in brick that still stand; the present structure dates from the 5th–6th century.","e":"It marks where the Buddha attained enlightenment; UNESCO listed it in 2002.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1578","q":"Which of the following is a stone-like disc seen at the top of the Hindu temple, mostly in north Indian temples?","o":["Garbhagriha","Amalaka","Jagati","Antarala"],"a":1,"x":"The amalaka is the ribbed stone disc on top of the shikhara of Nagara (north Indian) temples, with a kalasha above it.","e":"Garbhagriha: sanctum. Antarala: vestibule. Jagati: raised platform.","src":["Patwari 18 Sep 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q1590","q":"In Buddhist architecture what is the meaning of Chaitya Hall?","o":["Recreation Hall","Place of Worship","Hall of Spiritual Leaders","Place for Marriage Ceremony"],"a":1,"x":"A chaitya is a Buddhist prayer hall (place of worship) with a stupa at its far end; a vihara is a monastery where monks live.","e":"Karla and Bhaja (Maharashtra) have famous rock-cut chaityas.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1600","q":"The 'Bani Thani' painting is associated with which school?","o":["Kishangarh","Mewar","Bundi","Bikaner"],"a":0,"x":"'Bani Thani', called 'India's Mona Lisa', belongs to the Kishangarh school of Rajasthani painting and was painted by Nihal Chand in the 18th century.","e":"A 1973 Indian postage stamp showed Bani Thani.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q0482","q":"Which among the following is also known as \"Harmandir Sahib\"?","o":["Bangla Sahib, Delhi","Golden Temple, Punjab","San Sahib, Punjab","Jagannath Temple, Odisha"],"a":1,"x":"Harmandir Sahib is the Golden Temple at Amritsar, the holiest Sikh gurdwara, built under Guru Arjan Dev (foundation 1588).","e":"Maharaja Ranjit Singh covered it in gold in the 19th century.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0521","q":"The Shalimar Garden of Jammu and Kashmir was built by which of the following Mughal emperor?","o":["Aurangzeb","Jahangir","Shah Jahan","Babur"],"a":1,"x":"The Shalimar Bagh in Srinagar was built by Jahangir in 1619 for Nur Jahan.","e":"Nishat Bagh was built by Asaf Khan, Nur Jahan's brother.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0601","q":"In which of the following states is the Kumbhalgarh Fort located?","o":["Maharashtra","Tamil Nadu","Rajasthan","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"Kumbhalgarh Fort (15th century, Rana Kumbha) in Rajsamand district, Rajasthan, has one of the world's longest walls (about 36 km); it is a UNESCO Hill Fort of Rajasthan.","e":"Maharana Pratap was born here.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0641","q":"Who among the following built the Red Fort in Delhi?","o":["Shah Jahan","Akbar","Babur","Humayun"],"a":0,"x":"Shah Jahan built the Red Fort (Lal Qila) in Delhi between 1639 and 1648 when he moved the capital to Shahjahanabad.","e":"The Prime Minister hoists the flag here on Independence Day; it is a UNESCO site.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0681","q":"Rani ki Vav or 'Queen's Stepwell' is a unique stepwell located in which of the following states?","o":["Gujarat","Madhya Pradesh","Maharashtra","Tamil Nadu"],"a":0,"x":"Rani ki Vav at Patan, Gujarat, was built in the 11th century by Queen Udayamati in memory of Bhimdev I of the Chaulukya (Solanki) dynasty; UNESCO listed it in 2014.","e":"It appears on the back of the ₹100 note.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0724","q":"Jhulta Minar is located in which one of the following states of India?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Gujarat","Andhra Pradesh","Rajasthan"],"a":1,"x":"The Jhulta Minar (shaking minarets) of the Sidi Bashir Mosque is in Ahmedabad, Gujarat: shake one minaret and the other shakes too.","e":"Ahmedabad was India's first UNESCO World Heritage City (2017).","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1738","q":"Raigad Fort is located in which state?","o":["Rajasthan","Uttar Pradesh","Maharashtra","Telangana"],"a":2,"x":"Raigad Fort in Maharashtra was Shivaji's capital; he was crowned there in 1674.","e":"Shivaji: born at Shivneri, crowned at Raigad.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1740","q":"Khazana Building Museum is located in _____________.","o":["New Delhi","Hyderabad","Aurangabad","Mumbai"],"a":1,"x":"The Khazana Building Museum near Golconda, Hyderabad (Telangana), displays sculptures from the Kakatiya and Chalukya periods.","e":"Golconda fort is in Hyderabad too.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1758","q":"Who constructed the first floor of Qutab Minar?","o":["Qutabuddin Aybak","Iltumish","Bahlul Lodi","Alauddin Khalji"],"a":0,"x":"Qutbuddin Aibak built the first storey of the Qutub Minar (about 1199); Iltutmish added three storeys and Firoz Shah Tughlaq repaired the top.","e":"Aibak started it, Iltutmish finished it.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1782","q":"Which of the following monument is NOT located in Delhi?","o":["Kotla Feroz Shah","Humayun’s Tomb","Safadarjung Tomb","Fatehpur Sikri"],"a":3,"x":"Fatehpur Sikri is near Agra in Uttar Pradesh. Feroz Shah Kotla, Humayun's Tomb and Safdarjung's Tomb are in Delhi.","e":"Fatehpur Sikri was Akbar's capital (1571–85).","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1784","q":"‘Kamakhya temple’ is located in which city?","o":["Bengaluru","Guwahati","New Delhi","Jaipur"],"a":1,"x":"The Kamakhya temple stands on Nilachal hill in Guwahati, Assam; it is one of the most important Shakti peeths.","e":"The Ambubachi Mela is held there every June.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1822","q":"Which of the following monument is located in Rajasthan? I. Bellary Fort II. Amer Fort","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Amer (Amber) Fort near Jaipur is in Rajasthan; Bellary Fort is in Karnataka, so only II is correct.","e":"Amer is part of the UNESCO 'Hill Forts of Rajasthan'.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1934","q":"Khazan Building Museum is located in which state?","o":["Kerala","Telangana","Chhattisgarh","Rajasthan"],"a":1,"x":"The Khazana Building Museum is near Golconda in Hyderabad, Telangana; it holds sculptures and inscriptions from the Kakatiya and other dynasties.","e":"Telangana's capital is Hyderabad.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1936","q":"Which of the following monument(s) is/are located in Gujarat? I. Lakhota fort II. Sun temple of Modhera III. Pavagadh fort","o":["II and III","Only II","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"All three are in Gujarat: Lakhota Fort (Jamnagar), the Modhera Sun Temple (built by Solanki king Bhima I, 1026) and Pavagadh fort, part of the UNESCO Champaner–Pavagadh park.","e":"Modhera Sun Temple was built by the Solankis.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1975","q":"______ is located in Himachal Pradesh.","o":["Golkonda fort","Kangra fort","Warangal fort","Kumbhalgarh fort"],"a":1,"x":"Kangra Fort, one of India's oldest forts, is in Himachal Pradesh. Golconda: Telangana; Warangal: Telangana; Kumbhalgarh: Rajasthan.","e":"Kumbhalgarh has the second-longest continuous wall in the world.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2020","q":"Rahim-Khane-Khanam Tomb is located in ______.","o":["Delhi","Ranchi","Punjab","Agartala"],"a":0,"x":"The tomb of Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan, a Navaratna of Akbar's court, is in Nizamuddin, Delhi, near Humayun's Tomb.","e":"Rahim is known for his Hindi dohas.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2022","q":"Which of the following monument(s) is/are located in Uttar Pradesh? I. Tomb of Janab-e-Aliya II. Lord Cornwallis Tomb","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Both are in Uttar Pradesh: Lord Cornwallis's tomb is at Ghazipur (he died there in 1805) and the Tomb of Janab-e-Aliya is in Lucknow.","e":"Cornwallis: Permanent Settlement (1793).","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2062","q":"Kashi Vishwanath temple is located in _____.","o":["Chandigarh","Ahmadabad","Varanasi","Guwahati"],"a":2,"x":"The Kashi Vishwanath temple, one of the 12 jyotirlingas of Shiva, is in Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh.","e":"The Kashi Vishwanath Corridor opened in 2021.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2142","q":"Which of the following is known as the last resting place of the Mughal emperor Akbar?","o":["Red Fort","Sikandra Fort","Purana Qila","Agra Fort"],"a":1,"x":"Akbar's tomb is at Sikandra near Agra; construction began under Akbar and Jahangir completed it in 1613.","e":"Humayun's tomb: Delhi; Jahangir's: Lahore; Shah Jahan's: Taj Mahal.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2144","q":"From which of the following places was the biggest rock-cut chaitya-hall excavated?","o":["Bedsa","Nashik","Karla","Kanheri"],"a":2,"x":"The Karla (Karle) chaitya hall near Lonavala, Maharashtra (1st century BCE–CE), is the largest rock-cut chaitya hall in India.","e":"Chaitya = prayer hall; vihara = monastery.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2387","q":"What is the another name of Golden Temple?","o":["Shri Singh Sahib","Guru Ramdas Sahab","Shri Arjun Sahib","Shri Darbar Sahib"],"a":3,"x":"The Golden Temple in Amritsar is also called Sri Harmandir Sahib or Sri Darbar Sahib; Guru Arjan Dev completed it.","e":"The Adi Granth was installed there in 1604.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2389","q":"In which of the following districts 'Bara Imambara' is situated?","o":["Hyderabad","Bhopal","Kashmir","Lucknow"],"a":3,"x":"The Bara Imambara in Lucknow was built by Nawab Asaf-ud-Daula in 1784 and is famous for its maze, the Bhool Bhulaiya.","e":"Rumi Darwaza stands next to it.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2431","q":"In which year was the Kandariya Mahadeva Temple constructed?","o":["999 AD","1065 AD","1100 AD","1099 AD"],"a":0,"x":"The Kandariya Mahadeva temple at Khajuraho (Madhya Pradesh) was built around 999 CE by the Chandela kings (NCERT Class 7).","e":"Khajuraho temples are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2432","q":"Where is the Shalimar Gardens, built by Jehangir, situated in India?","o":["Hyderabad","Delhi","Srinagar","Daulatabad"],"a":2,"x":"Jahangir built the Shalimar Bagh in Srinagar for Nur Jahan (1619) on the shore of the Dal Lake.","e":"Nishat Bagh (also Srinagar) was built by Asaf Khan.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2476","q":"In which of the following places is the Raniji ki baori?","o":["Kota","Bundi","Jhalawar","Tonk"],"a":1,"x":"Raniji ki Baori, a 46-m-deep stepwell built in 1699 by Rani Nathavati, is in Bundi, Rajasthan.","e":"Bundi is called the 'city of stepwells'.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2516","q":"Which of the following dynasties had constructed various chahar baghs in India?","o":["Lodhis","Tughlaqs","Mughals","Khiljis"],"a":2,"x":"The Mughals built chahar baghs, four-part walled gardens divided by water channels, e.g. at Humayun's Tomb and the Taj Mahal (NCERT Class 7).","e":"'Chahar' = four.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2517","q":"In which of the following places is the Thousand Pillars Temple located?","o":["Hanamkonda","Karimnagar","Guntur","Kadapa"],"a":0,"x":"The Thousand Pillar Temple (Rudreshwara) at Hanamkonda, Telangana, was built by Kakatiya king Rudra Deva in 1163.","e":"Kakatiya capital: Warangal.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2591","q":"Which of the following pairs of state monument is/are correct? I. Bihar – Shershah Suri’s Tomb II. West Bengal – Shaheed Minar","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Sher Shah Suri's tomb is at Sasaram in Bihar, and the Shaheed Minar (Ochterlony Monument) is in Kolkata, West Bengal. Both pairs are correct.","e":"Sher Shah's tomb stands in the middle of a lake.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2658","q":"Sultan Ghari’s Tomb is located in ______.","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Kerala","Delhi","Bihar"],"a":2,"x":"Sultan Ghari (1231), built by Iltutmish for his son Nasiruddin Mahmud, in Delhi, is India's first Islamic tomb.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1304","q":"In which of the following states is the Chandragad fort situated?","o":["Karnataka","Andhra Pradesh","Maharashtra","Gujarat"],"a":2,"x":"Chandragad Fort is in the Sahyadris (Western Ghats) of Maharashtra, in Satara district, associated with the Marathas.","e":"Maharashtra has more than 350 forts; Raigad was Shivaji's capital.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1344","q":"Who among the following built the Vijaya Stambha of Rajasthan?","o":["Pratap Singh","Sawai Jai Singh","Surajmal Singh","Maharana Kumbha"],"a":3,"x":"The Vijaya Stambha (Victory Tower) at Chittorgarh was built by Rana Kumbha (around 1448) to celebrate his victory over Mahmud Khilji of Malwa.","e":"The nearby Kirti Stambha is a Jain tower.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0895","q":"Which of the following religions is associated with the Gangaramaya Temple of Sri Lanka?","o":["Islam","Hinduism","Buddhism","Sikhism"],"a":2,"x":"Gangaramaya Temple in Colombo is one of Sri Lanka's most important Buddhist temples.","e":"Sri Lanka's majority religion is Theravada Buddhism.","src":["PRT 16 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0905","q":"Which of the following buildings in Fatehpur Sikri is a fine example of ancient Indian architecture?","o":["Jodha Bai Palace","Dash Mahal","Hawa Mahal","Raja Mahal"],"a":0,"x":"Jodha Bai's Palace (Mariam-uz-Zamani's palace) at Fatehpur Sikri blends traditional Hindu and Gujarati temple architecture with Mughal style.","e":"Fatehpur Sikri was built by Akbar; Buland Darwaza marks his Gujarat victory.","src":["PRT 17 Feb 2026 S3"],"k":"The question's word 'ancient' really means traditional Indian style; the palace itself is Mughal, 16th century."},{"id":"q0906","q":"The Kailasa temple at Ellora, viewed as a grand specimen of Rashtrakuta artistry, was built under the patronage of which king?","o":["Indra Varma","Vikramaditya","Krishna II","Krishna I"],"a":3,"x":"The Kailasa (Kailashnath) temple, Cave 16 at Ellora, was cut from a single rock under the Rashtrakuta king Krishna I in the 8th century.","e":"It is the world's largest monolithic rock-cut structure.","src":["PRT 17 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0986","q":"Which monument, built by Shah Jahan in memory of his wife Mumtaz Mahal, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site?","o":["Red Fort","Taj Mahal","Qutub Minar","Humayun's Tomb"],"a":1,"x":"Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal at Agra (1632–53) in memory of Mumtaz Mahal; UNESCO listed it in 1983.","e":"Chief architect: Ustad Ahmad Lahori.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1014","q":"Pattachitra paintings are traditionally associated with which deity and region?","o":["Lord Jagannath, Odisha","Lord Shiva, Tamil Nadu","Lord Krishna, Uttar Pradesh","Goddess Durga, West Bengal"],"a":0,"x":"Pattachitra is traditional cloth-scroll painting of Odisha (and Bengal), closely tied to Lord Jagannath of Puri.","e":"Raghurajpur village in Odisha is famous for it.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1034","q":"The Qutub Minar, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is located in which Indian city?","o":["Delhi","Hyderabad","Jaipur","Agra"],"a":0,"x":"The Qutub Minar (about 73 m) in Mehrauli, Delhi, begun by Qutbuddin Aibak and completed by Iltutmish, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site (1993).","e":"Delhi's three UNESCO sites: Qutub Minar, Red Fort, Humayun's Tomb.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1086","q":"The Vesara style of temple architecture is a fusion of which two architectural styles?","o":["Dravida and Hoysala","Nagara and Dravida","Nagara and Kalinga","Dravida and Kalinga"],"a":1,"x":"The Vesara style, developed under the later Chalukyas and Hoysalas in the Deccan, combines the Nagara (northern) and Dravida (southern) temple styles.","e":"Nagara = north, Dravida = south, Vesara = mix (Deccan).","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1107","q":"The Warli painting tradition originated in which Indian state?","o":["Odisha","Rajasthan","West Bengal","Maharashtra"],"a":3,"x":"Warli painting comes from the Warli tribe of Maharashtra's Thane–Palghar region, drawn with white rice paste as simple circles, triangles and lines on mud walls.","e":"Madhubani is from Bihar; Pattachitra from Odisha.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1149","q":"The Brihadeeswarar Temple at Thanjavur, known for its towering vimana, was built under which dynasty?","o":["Pallava","Vijayanagara","Chola","Hoysala"],"a":2,"x":"The Brihadeeswarar (Rajarajeshwaram) Temple at Thanjavur was built by Chola king Rajaraja I around 1010 CE; its vimana is about 66 m high.","e":"UNESCO 'Great Living Chola Temples' with Gangaikondacholapuram and Darasuram.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1166","q":"The Ajanta Caves, known for their exquisite murals and sculptures, are located in which Indian state?","o":["Gujarat","Karnataka","Maharashtra","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"The Ajanta caves (2nd century BCE – 6th century CE) near Aurangabad, Maharashtra, have Buddhist murals and sculptures; UNESCO site since 1983.","e":"Ajanta = Buddhist paintings; Ellora = Buddhist, Hindu and Jain.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1192","q":"Jaisalmer Fort, built by Rawal Jaisal on Trikuta Hill, has how many layers of walls?","o":["four","three","two","five"],"a":1,"x":"Jaisalmer Fort (Sonar Qila, 1156 CE, Rawal Jaisal) on Trikuta Hill has three layers of walls and is a 'living fort' with residents inside.","e":"It is one of UNESCO's Hill Forts of Rajasthan.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1209","q":"Which traditional Indian painting style is known for intricate patterns and is often made on cloth using natural dyes?","o":["Madhubani","Warli","Pattachitra","Kalighat"],"a":2,"x":"Pattachitra (from Odisha and Bengal) is painted on cloth with natural colours and known for intricate borders and patterns, mostly on Jagannath themes.","e":"Madhubani = Bihar; Warli = Maharashtra; Kalighat = Kolkata.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1271","q":"During which century was the Quwwat al-Islam mosque and minaret built in Delhi?","o":["Tenth century","Twelfth century","Eleventh century","Fifteenth century"],"a":1,"x":"The Quwwat-ul-Islam mosque and the Qutub Minar in Delhi were begun by Qutbuddin Aibak in the late 12th century (from 1193).","e":"Iltutmish completed the Qutub Minar; Alauddin Khalji added the Alai Darwaza.","src":["PRT 10 Apr 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1364","q":"Sultan Garhi, built in 1231 for Prince Nasiruddin and regarded as India's first Islamic tomb, is located in which city?","o":["Panipat","Lucknow","Delhi","Agra"],"a":2,"x":"Sultan Garhi (1231), built by Iltutmish for his eldest son Prince Nasiruddin Mahmud, is regarded as India's first Islamic tomb; it is in Delhi.","e":"Iltutmish's own tomb is in the Qutub complex.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1387","q":"Which of the following types of structures is NOT associated with Buddhist architecture in India?","o":["Chaityas","Monkas","Viharas","Stupas"],"a":1,"x":"Buddhist architecture in India includes stupas (relic mounds), chaityas (prayer halls) and viharas (monasteries). 'Monkas' is not a Buddhist structure.","e":"Karla has a famous rock-cut chaitya hall.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1420","q":"The temples at Khajuraho, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, were built during the reign of which of the following dynasties?","o":["Maurya","Chandela","Pallava","Kakatiya"],"a":1,"x":"The Khajuraho temples (950–1050 CE) in Madhya Pradesh were built by the Chandela dynasty; UNESCO listed them in 1986.","e":"The Kandariya Mahadeva is the largest Khajuraho temple.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1422","q":"Which of the following Indian monuments is NOT a UNESCO World Heritage Site?","o":["Red Fort","Char Minar","Taj Mahal","Humayun’s Tomb"],"a":1,"x":"The Charminar in Hyderabad is not a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The Red Fort, Taj Mahal and Humayun's Tomb are.","e":"The Charminar was built by Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah in 1591.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1427","q":"The Ajanta caves of Maharashtra are cave monuments connected with which religion?","o":["Zoroastrianism","Buddhism","Hinduism","Jainism"],"a":1,"x":"The Ajanta Caves (2nd century BCE – 6th century CE) in Maharashtra are Buddhist rock-cut monasteries and prayer halls, famous for their murals.","e":"Ellora has Buddhist, Hindu and Jain caves.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1439","q":"Which of the following monuments is NOT located in Delhi?","o":["Qutub Minar","Alai Minar","Balban's Tomb","Bibi ka Maqbara"],"a":3,"x":"Bibi ka Maqbara, the 'Taj of the Deccan', is at Aurangabad (Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar), Maharashtra, built by Azam Shah for his mother. The Qutub Minar, Alai Minar and Balban's Tomb are in Delhi.","e":"Balban's Tomb in Mehrauli has the first true arch in India.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1463","q":"The dance hall in the Sun Temple at Konark is referred to as _______.","o":["Jamagohana","Nat Mandir","Shikhara","Vimana"],"a":1,"x":"The dance hall (Nata Mandira) of the Konark Sun Temple stands in front of the main temple on a high platform.","e":"The jagamohana (assembly hall) is the part still standing.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q0815","q":"The ancient Buddhist stupa, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, located in Madhya Pradesh, is:","o":["Sanchi Stupa","Ajanta Caves","Elephanta Caves","Mahabodhi Temple"],"a":0,"x":"The Great Stupa at Sanchi in Raisen district, Madhya Pradesh, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site (1989).","e":"Ajanta and Elephanta are in Maharashtra; Mahabodhi Temple in Bihar.","src":["PGT 22 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0826","q":"Which of the following cities is home to the famous Sun Temple, a UNESCO World Heritage Site?","o":["Hampi","Jaipur","Agra","Konark"],"a":3,"x":"The Sun Temple at Konark, Odisha (13th century, Narasimhadeva I), is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","e":"It is built as Surya's chariot, with 24 wheels and 7 horses.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]}]},{"id":"books","n":"Books and Authors","qs":[{"id":"q0265","q":"Which of the following is the first novel in English written by an Indian author?","o":["Anandamath","Mritunjay","The Guide","Rajmohan’s Wife"],"a":3,"x":"Rajmohan's Wife (1864) by Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay is the first novel in English by an Indian author.","e":"Bankim also wrote Anandamath (1882), which contains 'Vande Mataram'.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0273","q":"'The Autobiography of an Unknown Indian', a significant work in the history of Indian literature, was written by which prominent figure?","o":["Nirad C Chaudhuri","Khushwant Singh","RK Narayan","VS Naipaul"],"a":0,"x":"'The Autobiography of an Unknown Indian' (1951) was written by Nirad C. Chaudhuri.","e":"Also by him: 'Thy Hand, Great Anarch!'","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0277","q":"Which of the following books was written by Ram Manohar Lohia, critiquing the role of various leaders in the partition of India?","o":["An Autobiography of an Unknown Indian","Guilty Men of India’s Partition","India Wins Freedom","The Story of My Experiments with Truth"],"a":1,"x":"Ram Manohar Lohia wrote 'Guilty Men of India's Partition' (1960), blaming leaders across parties for Partition.","e":"'India Wins Freedom': Maulana Azad. 'The Story of My Experiments with Truth': Gandhi.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0306","q":"Which literary magazine started by Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay in 1872 played an important role in promoting Bengali nationalism?","o":["Bharati","Desh","Anandabazar Patrika","Bangadarshan"],"a":3,"x":"Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay started the Bengali monthly 'Bangadarshan' in 1872; it promoted Bengali literature and nationalism.","e":"Anandamath was first serialised in Bangadarshan.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0546","q":"Which among the following books is not written by Chetan Bhagat?","o":["Revolution 2020","One Indian Girl","What Young India Wants","Sons and Lovers"],"a":3,"x":"'Sons and Lovers' (1913) is by D. H. Lawrence. Chetan Bhagat wrote 'Revolution 2020', 'One Indian Girl' and 'What Young India Wants'.","e":"Chetan Bhagat's first novel: 'Five Point Someone' (2004).","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0585","q":"Who is the author of 'India Divided'?","o":["Jawaharlal Nehru","Rajendra Prasad","Shashi Tharoor","A. P. J. Abdul Kalam"],"a":1,"x":"'India Divided' (1946) was written by Dr Rajendra Prasad, arguing against the two-nation theory.","e":"His autobiography: 'Atmakatha'.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0625","q":"'Mind Master' is an autobiography of which of the following persons?","o":["Viswanathan Anand","Sachin Tendulkar","Bhagat Singh","Sania Mirza"],"a":0,"x":"'Mind Master: Winning Lessons from a Champion's Life' (2019) is by chess champion Viswanathan Anand, with Susan Ninan.","e":"Anand was India's first Grandmaster (1988).","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0665","q":"'Obama: The Call of History' is written by ____________.","o":["Barack Obama","Peter Baker","Donald Trump","Michelle Obama"],"a":1,"x":"'Obama: The Call of History' (2017) was written by journalist Peter Baker of The New York Times.","e":"Obama's own memoir: 'A Promised Land' (2020).","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0705","q":"Which among the following books is written by Salman Rushdie?","o":["The King of Dark Chamber","The End of Imagination","Between the Assassinations","The Enchantress of Florence"],"a":3,"x":"'The Enchantress of Florence' (2008) is by Salman Rushdie. 'The King of the Dark Chamber' is by Tagore, 'The End of Imagination' by Arundhati Roy, and 'Between the Assassinations' by Aravind Adiga.","e":"Rushdie's 'Midnight's Children' won the Booker Prize in 1981.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0746","q":"Which one of the following is not a literary work by Munshi Premchand?","o":["Shatranj ke Khiladi","Nirmala","Anamdas ka Potha","Gaban"],"a":2,"x":"'Anamdas ka Potha' is by Hazari Prasad Dwivedi. Premchand wrote 'Shatranj ke Khiladi', 'Nirmala' and 'Gaban'.","e":"Premchand's last complete novel: 'Godaan' (1936).","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1761","q":"‘Lessons Life Taught Me Unknowingly’ is an autobiography of _____________.","o":["Venkaiah Naidu","Manoj Kumar","Anupam Kher","Viswanathan Anand"],"a":2,"x":"'Lessons Life Taught Me Unknowingly' (2019) is the autobiography of actor Anupam Kher.","e":"Kher also wrote 'The Best Thing About You Is You'.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S3","Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1805","q":"‘Listening, Learning and Leading’ book launched by Amit Shah is based on whom?","o":["Swami Ramdev","Narendra Modi","M. Venkaiah Naidu","Sachin Tendulkar"],"a":2,"x":"'Listening, Learning and Leading' (2019), released by Amit Shah, records Vice-President M. Venkaiah Naidu's second year in office.","e":"Naidu was Vice-President from 2017 to 2022.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1846","q":"Who is the author of the book ‘The Unquiet River: A Biography of the Brahmaputra’?","o":["Elif Shafak","Ramachandra Guha","Arupjyoti Saikia","Arundhati Roy"],"a":2,"x":"'The Unquiet River: A Biography of the Brahmaputra' (2019) is by historian Arupjyoti Saikia.","e":"The Brahmaputra is called the Tsangpo in Tibet and Jamuna in Bangladesh.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1997","q":"Who is the author of the book ‘The Ickabog’?","o":["J.K. Rowling","Ruskin Bond","John Sandford","Leone Ross"],"a":0,"x":"'The Ickabog' (2020), a children's fairy tale, was written by J.K. Rowling, author of the Harry Potter series.","e":"It was first published free online during the COVID-19 lockdown.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2044","q":"The book titled ‘Vijyant at Kargil: The Life of a Kargil Hero’ is written by ______.","o":["B.B. Lal","V.N. Thapar and Neha Dwivedi","Harsh Vardhan","R.N. Thapar and Mridula Banerjee"],"a":1,"x":"'Vijyant at Kargil: The Life of a Kargil Hero' was written by Col. V.N. Thapar and Neha Dwivedi about Captain Vijyant Thapar, who was killed in the 1999 Kargil War.","e":"Kargil War: Operation Vijay, 1999.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2085","q":"Who is the author of the book ‘Politics of Jugaad: The Coalition Handbook’?","o":["Gurcharan Das","Saba Naqvi","Vikram Seth","Anita Desai"],"a":1,"x":"'Politics of Jugaad: The Coalition Handbook' (2018) is by journalist Saba Naqvi.","e":"Her other book: 'Shades of Saffron'.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2089","q":"Who has written the book ‘Early Indians’?","o":["Tony Joseph","Margaret Atwood","George Saunders","Anna Burns"],"a":0,"x":"'Early Indians: The Story of Our Ancestors and Where We Came From' (2018) is by journalist Tony Joseph.","e":"It draws on ancient DNA research.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2122","q":"‘Playing with Fire’ is the autobiography of which cricketer?","o":["James Anderson","Nasser Hussain","Lasith Malinga","Shane Warne"],"a":1,"x":"'Playing with Fire' is the autobiography of former England cricket captain Nasser Hussain.","e":"Shane Warne's autobiography: 'No Spin'.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2126","q":"Who is the author of the book ‘The Third Pillar’?","o":["Y.V. Reddy","Raghuram Rajan","Urjit Patel","Shaktikanta Das"],"a":1,"x":"'The Third Pillar: How Markets and the State Leave the Community Behind' (2019) is by former RBI Governor Raghuram Rajan.","e":"Rajan also wrote 'I Do What I Do'.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2404","q":"In which language was the epic 'Silappadikaram' composed by a poet named Ilango?","o":["Malayalam","Tamil","Kannada","Telugu"],"a":1,"x":"'Silappadikaram', the Tamil epic about Kannagi and Kovalan, was composed by Ilango Adigal.","e":"Its companion epic is 'Manimekalai' by Sattanar.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2494","q":"Who had composed the Tamil epic called Manimekalai?","o":["Sattanar","Kovalan","Ilango","Kambar"],"a":0,"x":"The Tamil epic 'Manimekalai' was composed by Sattanar; it continues the story of 'Silappadikaram' by Ilango.","e":"Manimekalai is the daughter of Kovalan and Madhavi.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2539","q":"Who is the author of the book 'The mysterious stranger'?","o":["Charles Dickens","Mark Twain","Oscar Wilde","Jack London"],"a":1,"x":"'The Mysterious Stranger' is a novel by Mark Twain, published after his death (1916).","e":"Twain also wrote 'Tom Sawyer' and 'Huckleberry Finn'.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2613","q":"Which of the following books is written by Pranab Mukherjee?","o":["Pax Indica","The Coalition Years","Why I am a Hindu","Exam Warrior"],"a":1,"x":"'The Coalition Years 1996–2012' is by former President Pranab Mukherjee.","e":"His other books: 'The Dramatic Decade', 'The Turbulent Years'.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2682","q":"Who is the author of the book ‘Song of India’?","o":["Shashi Tharoor","Ruskin Bond","Anna Burns","Meenakshi Lekhi"],"a":1,"x":"'Song of India' (2020) is by Ruskin Bond, part of his Rusty series.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1316","q":"Who is the author of the book \"The God of Small Things\"?","o":["Jhumpa Lahiri","Arvind Adiga","Karan Thapar","Arundhati Roy"],"a":3,"x":"'The God of Small Things' (1997) is by Arundhati Roy, who won the Booker Prize for it.","e":"Aravind Adiga won the Booker in 2008 for 'The White Tiger'.","src":["PGT 22 Jul 2021","Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1321","q":"In which of the following languages were the Mangalkavyas written?","o":["Braj","Pali","Bengali","Hindi"],"a":2,"x":"Mangalkavyas are medieval Bengali narrative poems praising deities such as Manasa, Chandi and Dharma.","e":"Example: Mukundaram's 'Chandimangal'.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1352","q":"Which among the following books is not written by Rudyard Kipling?","o":["Midnight's Children","Under the Deodars","In Black and White","The Jungle Book"],"a":0,"x":"'Midnight's Children' (1981) is by Salman Rushdie. Rudyard Kipling wrote 'Under the Deodars', 'In Black and White' and 'The Jungle Book'.","e":"Kipling won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1907.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1362","q":"Who among the following is the author of 'To Kill a Mockingbird'?","o":["Agatha Christie","Ernest Hemingway","Harper Lee","Arthur Conan Doyle"],"a":2,"x":"'To Kill a Mockingbird' (1960) is by the American author Harper Lee; it won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1961.","e":"Ernest Hemingway: 'The Old Man and the Sea'. Arthur Conan Doyle: Sherlock Holmes. Agatha Christie: Hercule Poirot.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1472","q":"Who composed the famous Tamil epic Silappadikaram?","o":["Kovalan","Thiruvalluvar","Sattanar","Ilango"],"a":3,"x":"Silappadikaram, one of the Five Great Epics of Tamil literature, was composed by Ilango Adigal; it tells the story of Kannagi and Kovalan.","e":"Its sequel, Manimekalai, is by Sattanar; Thiruvalluvar wrote the Tirukkural.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q1539","q":"The famous book 'Pataliputra ki Samragyi' is associated with ________.","o":["Mamta Kalia","Sharad Pagare","Mannu Bhandari","Narendra Kohli"],"a":1,"x":"The Hindi novel 'Pataliputra ki Samragyi' is by Sharad Pagare.","e":"His novel 'Pehla Satyagrahi' is about Kalinga's Kharavela.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1549","q":"Who is the author of the book 'The Argumentative Indian', which explores India's cultural history and diversity?","o":["Salman Rushdie","Arundhati Roy","Amartya Sen","Jhumpa Lahiri"],"a":2,"x":"'The Argumentative Indian' (2005) is by Amartya Sen, who celebrates India's tradition of public debate.","e":"Also by Sen: 'Development as Freedom', 'The Idea of Justice'.","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]}]},{"id":"sports","n":"Sports","qs":[{"id":"q0517","q":"Who among the following holds the record of most wins at the Women's World Team Badminton Championships for the Uber Cup as of 31st March 2020?","o":["United States","Indonesia","Japan","China"],"a":3,"x":"China has won the Uber Cup (women's world team badminton) more often than any other country.","e":"The Thomas Cup is the men's event; India won it for the first time in 2022.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0518","q":"The first three Cricket World Cups were also called ___________ World Cups.","o":["Ecstatic","Prudential","Primary","Historical"],"a":1,"x":"The first three Cricket World Cups (1975, 1979, 1983), all in England, were sponsored by the Prudential insurance company and called the Prudential Cups.","e":"India won the 1983 Prudential Cup under Kapil Dev.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0558","q":"Which among the following teams won the first edition of the ICC Women's T20 World Cup in 2009?","o":["England","Australia","India","New Zealand"],"a":0,"x":"England won the first ICC Women's T20 World Cup in 2009, beating New Zealand in the final at Lord's.","e":"Pakistan won the men's T20 World Cup the same year.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0598","q":"Which among the following won the first FIFA World Cup held in 1930?","o":["Yugoslavia","Argentina","Uruguay","United States"],"a":2,"x":"Uruguay hosted and won the first FIFA World Cup in 1930, beating Argentina 4–2 in the final.","e":"Most titles: Brazil (5).","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0637","q":"Who among the following hosted the first one-day Cricket World Cup tournament held in 1975?","o":["England","Australia","India","New Zealand"],"a":0,"x":"England hosted the first Cricket World Cup in 1975 (and the next two); West Indies won the first two.","e":"India won in 1983 and 2011.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0680","q":"With which of the following sports Corbillon Cup is associated?","o":["Badminton","Cricket","Golf","Table Tennis"],"a":3,"x":"The Corbillon Cup is the women's team trophy of the World Table Tennis Championships.","e":"The men's equivalent is the Swaythling Cup.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0718","q":"Who among the following Indian women has scored the highest aggregate runs in the Women's ODI cricket format as of April 2020?","o":["Harmanpreet Kaur","Smriti Mandhana","Mithali Raj","Deepti Sharma"],"a":2,"x":"Mithali Raj is the highest run-scorer in women's ODI cricket (7,805 runs), a record she held on retiring in 2022.","e":"She was the first woman to cross 7,000 ODI runs.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0720","q":"The Beighton Cup is associated with which of the following sports?","o":["Cricket","Golf","Table Tennis","Hockey"],"a":3,"x":"The Beighton Cup (1895, Kolkata) is one of the world's oldest field hockey tournaments.","e":"Other hockey trophies: Aga Khan Cup, Rangaswamy Cup, Dhyan Chand Trophy.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0756","q":"As of April 2020, who is the highest wicket-taker in one day international in women's cricket?","o":["Ellyse Perry","Catherine Fitzpatrick","Jhulan Goswami","Marizanne Kapp"],"a":2,"x":"Jhulan Goswami is the highest wicket-taker in women's ODIs (255 wickets); she retired in 2022.","e":"She was ICC Women's Cricketer of the Year in 2007.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0758","q":"Which one of the following was the venue for the first edition of the Commonwealth Games in 1930?","o":["Hamilton, Canada","Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia","London, England","Sydney, Australia"],"a":0,"x":"The first Commonwealth Games (then British Empire Games) were held at Hamilton, Canada, in 1930.","e":"India hosted the 2010 Commonwealth Games in Delhi.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0759","q":"In which of the following games a player can also be called a raider?","o":["Hockey","Wrestling","Volleyball","Kabaddi"],"a":3,"x":"In kabaddi a raider enters the opponents' half chanting 'kabaddi', tries to touch defenders, and returns without being caught.","e":"Kabaddi team: 7 players on court.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0760","q":"Amongst the following, who was the first person to score a century for India in Test cricket?","o":["Lala Amarnath","Lalchand Rajput","Dilip Vengsarkar","Sunil Gavaskar"],"a":0,"x":"Lala Amarnath scored India's first Test century, 118 on debut against England at Bombay in 1933.","e":"Sunil Gavaskar was the first player to reach 10,000 Test runs.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1774","q":"How many total medals did India win at the 2018 Asian Games?","o":["56","61","69","78"],"a":2,"x":"India won 69 medals (15 gold, 24 silver, 30 bronze) at the 2018 Asian Games in Jakarta–Palembang, its best tally until 2023.","e":"2023 Hangzhou: 107 medals.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1775","q":"Which of the following pair of sports tournament – sport is correct? I. Davis Cup – Table Tennis II. Fed Cup – Tennis","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"The Davis Cup is a men's tennis team event (not table tennis); the Fed Cup, now the Billie Jean King Cup, is the women's tennis team event.","e":"Swaythling Cup: table tennis.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1776","q":"Which of the following is a sport of Summer Olympics? I. Handball II. Gymnastics Artistic III. Surfing","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"Handball and artistic gymnastics are long-standing Summer Olympic sports; surfing joined at Tokyo 2020.","e":"Tokyo 2020 also added skateboarding, sport climbing and karate.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1816","q":"Which of the following pair of sports – sportsperson is correct? I. Kabaddi – Manoj Tiwary II. Kabaddi – Ajay Thakur","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Ajay Thakur captained India's Kabaddi team to the 2016 World Cup; Manoj Tiwary is a cricketer, so only pair II is correct.","e":"Anup Kumar and Pardeep Narwal are other Kabaddi stars.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1817","q":"Which country hosted the FIH Women’s World Cup 2018?","o":["France","Belgium","England","Netherlands"],"a":2,"x":"England (London) hosted the 2018 FIH Women's Hockey World Cup; the Netherlands won it.","e":"Men's Hockey World Cup 2018 was in Bhubaneswar.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1818","q":"Which of the following pair of sports tournament – sport is correct? I. Uber Cup – Badminton II. Hopman Cup – Tennis","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The Uber Cup is the women's world team badminton championship and the Hopman Cup a mixed-team tennis event, so both pairs are correct.","e":"Thomas Cup: men's badminton (India won in 2022).","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1848","q":"Which former Indian cricket personality has become the first woman match referee to oversee a men’s ODI?","o":["G S Lakshmi","Shobha Pandit","Sujata Sridhar","Arundhati Ghosh"],"a":0,"x":"G.S. Lakshmi became the first woman to referee a men's ODI (2019) and the first woman on the ICC International Panel of Match Referees.","e":"She had played first-class cricket for South Zone.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1858","q":"Ramkumar Ramanathan is associated with which sport?","o":["Hockey","Tennis","Table Tennis","Wrestling"],"a":1,"x":"Ramkumar Ramanathan is an Indian tennis player from Chennai who plays mainly doubles and Davis Cup.","e":"Other Indian tennis names: Leander Paes, Rohan Bopanna, Sumit Nagal.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1860","q":"Who won the FIH Women’s World Cup 2018?","o":["Ireland","Australia","Netherlands","Spain"],"a":2,"x":"The Netherlands won the 2018 FIH Women's Hockey World Cup in London, beating Ireland in the final.","e":"The Netherlands are the most successful women's hockey nation.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1968","q":"Where is Bharat Ratna Shri Atal Bihari Vajpayee Ekana Cricket Stadium located?","o":["Lucknow","Kanpur","Greater Noida","Delhi"],"a":0,"x":"The Bharat Ratna Shri Atal Bihari Vajpayee Ekana Cricket Stadium is in Lucknow, Uttar Pradesh.","e":"Largest capacity in India: Narendra Modi Stadium, Ahmedabad.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1969","q":"How many silver medals did India won at the 2018 Asian Games?","o":["20","24","18","26"],"a":1,"x":"India won 24 silver medals at the 2018 Jakarta–Palembang Asian Games (15 gold, 24 silver, 30 bronze = 69).","e":"Total 69 in 2018; 107 in 2023.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1970","q":"Which of the following sports were played at 2020 Tokyo Olympics (played in 2021)? I. Badminton II. Archery","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Badminton and archery are both Summer Olympic sports and were contested at Tokyo 2020 (held in 2021).","e":"P.V. Sindhu won bronze in badminton at Tokyo.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2055","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the semi-finalist team in 2018 Men’s Hockey World Cup?","o":["Germany","Belgium","Netherlands","Australia"],"a":0,"x":"The 2018 Men's Hockey World Cup in Bhubaneswar had Belgium, the Netherlands, Australia and England as semi-finalists; Belgium won. Germany did not reach the semi-finals.","e":"Belgium's first World Cup title (2018).","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2056","q":"Which of the following sports was played at 2020 Tokyo Olympics (played in 2021)? I. Tennis II. Wrestling","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Tennis and wrestling were both Olympic sports at Tokyo 2020 (held in 2021); Ravi Kumar Dahiya won silver in wrestling.","e":"Bajrang Punia won wrestling bronze at Tokyo.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2057","q":"Which of the following is a domestic cricket tournament organised by BCCI? I. Syed Mushtaq Ali Trophy II. Vijay Hazare Trophy","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The Syed Mushtaq Ali Trophy (T20) and the Vijay Hazare Trophy (one-day) are BCCI domestic tournaments.","e":"Ranji Trophy: first-class; Duleep Trophy: zonal.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2097","q":"Vikas Gowda is associated with which sport?","o":["Discus Throw","Javelin Throw","Wrestling","Shooting"],"a":0,"x":"Vikas Gowda is an Indian discus thrower who won gold at the 2014 Commonwealth Games.","e":"Seema Punia is another discus thrower.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2098","q":"Which of the following pair of sports tournament – sport is correct? I. Concacaf Gold Cup – Shooting II. Cooch Bihar Trophy – Hockey","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"The CONCACAF Gold Cup is a football tournament and the Cooch Behar Trophy a BCCI under-19 cricket event, so both pairs are wrong.","e":"CONCACAF = North and Central America and Caribbean football.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2099","q":"Which of the following is NOT a sport of Summer Olympics?","o":["Sailing","Wushu","Water Polo","Sport Climbing"],"a":1,"x":"Wushu is not an Olympic sport (it appears at the Asian Games). Sailing, water polo and sport climbing are Summer Olympic sports.","e":"Sport climbing joined at Tokyo 2020.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2137","q":"Which of the following is NOT a sport of Summer Olympics?","o":["Trampoline","Rugby","Sepak takraw","Fencing"],"a":2,"x":"Sepak takraw (kick volleyball) is not an Olympic sport; it is played at the Asian Games. Trampoline, rugby sevens and fencing are Olympic events.","e":"Sepak takraw is popular in Southeast Asia.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2138","q":"Which of the following pair of sports – sportsperson is correct? I. Hockey – Mark Chapman II. Swimming – Toni Kroos","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Mark Chapman is a New Zealand cricketer and Toni Kroos a German footballer, so both pairs are wrong.","e":"Hockey greats: Dhyan Chand; swimming: Michael Phelps.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2179","q":"Who holds the record of highest bowling speed in cricket history?","o":["Brett Lee","Shane Bond","Shaun Tait","Shoaib Akhtar"],"a":3,"x":"Shoaib Akhtar of Pakistan bowled the fastest recorded delivery, 161.3 km/h, against England at the 2003 World Cup.","e":"He is nicknamed the 'Rawalpindi Express'.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2467","q":"With which of the following sports is Sushil Kumar related to?","o":["Hockey","Boxing","Wrestling","Archery"],"a":2,"x":"Sushil Kumar is a wrestler and India's only individual athlete with two Olympic medals in wrestling (bronze 2008, silver 2012).","e":"Yogeshwar Dutt: wrestling bronze, 2012.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2468","q":"With which of the following sports is the PGA Championship related to?","o":["Squash","Polo","Archery","Golf"],"a":3,"x":"The PGA Championship is one of golf's four men's major championships.","e":"Golf majors: Masters, PGA Championship, US Open, The Open.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2510","q":"Which of the following sports is Thomas Mueller related to?","o":["Football","Hockey","Tennis","Swimming"],"a":0,"x":"Thomas Müller is a German footballer who plays for Bayern Munich and won the 2014 World Cup.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2552","q":"With which of the following games is Manpreet Singh related to?","o":["Hockey","Wrestling","Golf","Boxing"],"a":0,"x":"Manpreet Singh is an Indian hockey player who captained India to bronze at the Tokyo 2020 Olympics.","e":"India's first Olympic hockey medal since 1980.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2553","q":"Which of the following countries does Luis Suarez play for?","o":["Brazil","Uruguay","Chile","Argentina"],"a":1,"x":"Luis Suárez is a Uruguayan footballer and Uruguay's all-time top scorer.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2622","q":"Where is the world's largest cricket stadium situated?","o":["Ahemdabad","Kolkata","Mumbai","Kanpur"],"a":0,"x":"The Narendra Modi Stadium (formerly Motera) in Ahmedabad is the world's largest cricket stadium, seating about 1,32,000.","e":"Second: Melbourne Cricket Ground.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2625","q":"Which of the following pair of sports – sportsperson is correct? I. Athletics – Andre Russell II. Motorsport – Usain Bolt","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Andre Russell is a West Indies cricketer and Usain Bolt a sprinter (athletics), so both pairs are wrong.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2626","q":"Which of the following pair of sports – sportsperson is correct? I. Tennis – Shafali Verma II. Cricket – Smriti Mandhana","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Shafali Verma and Smriti Mandhana are both Indian women cricketers, so only pair II is correct.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2693","q":"A hockey match consists of four quarters of ______ minutes.","o":["10","15","20","7.5"],"a":1,"x":"A hockey match has four quarters of 15 minutes each (FIH rules since 2014).","e":"Before 2014: two halves of 35 minutes.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2695","q":"Which of the following pair of sports – sportsperson is correct? I. Football – Sunil Chhetri II. Football – Dhanraj Pillay","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"Sunil Chhetri is India's football captain; Dhanraj Pillay is a hockey player, so only pair I is correct.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2696","q":"Which of the following pair of sports – sportsperson is correct? I. Hockey – Saikhom Mirabai Chanu II. Table Tennis – Ankita Raina","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Mirabai Chanu is a weightlifter (not hockey) and Ankita Raina a tennis player (not table tennis), so both pairs are wrong.","e":"Mirabai Chanu won silver at Tokyo 2020.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1308","q":"Which of the following teams did Pele play for?","o":["Argentina","Brazil","Spain","Portugal"],"a":1,"x":"Pelé played for Brazil and won three FIFA World Cups (1958, 1962, 1970), a record for a player.","e":"At club level he played for Santos (Brazil) and New York Cosmos.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1315","q":"With which of the following games was Prakash Amritraj associated?","o":["Badminton","Tennis","Football","Golf"],"a":1,"x":"Prakash Amritraj is a tennis player, son of Vijay Amritraj.","e":"Vijay and Anand Amritraj helped India reach the Davis Cup finals in 1974 and 1987.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1353","q":"Who among the following is the first cricket player to be awarded the Arjuna Award?","o":["Chandu Borde","Vijay Manjrekar","Mansoor Ali Khan Pataudi","Salim Durani"],"a":3,"x":"Salim Durani became the first cricketer to receive the Arjuna Award, in 1961.","e":"The Arjuna Awards began in 1961.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1355","q":"Which among the following cricket stadiums is not located in South Africa?","o":["Centurion Park","Kingsmead Stadium","Willowmoore Park","Sophia Gardens"],"a":3,"x":"Sophia Gardens is in Cardiff, Wales. Centurion Park, Kingsmead (Durban) and Willowmoore Park (Benoni) are in South Africa.","e":"Lord's in London is called the 'Home of Cricket'.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1357","q":"Ronjan Sodhi won the Rajiv Gandhi Khel Ratna in 2012 for his contribution in the field of _____________.","o":["wrestling","hockey","shooting","tennis"],"a":2,"x":"Ronjan Sodhi, a double-trap shooter, received the Rajiv Gandhi Khel Ratna (now Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna) in 2012.","e":"He won gold at the 2010 Asian Games.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1403","q":"Who among the following was the first woman president of the Indian Olympic Association?","o":["Sania Mirza","MC Mary Kom","PT Usha","Geeta Phogat"],"a":2,"x":"P. T. Usha was elected the first woman president of the Indian Olympic Association in December 2022.","e":"She is called the 'Payyoli Express'.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q0795","q":"Which of the following records is NOT related to Sachin Tendulkar?","o":["Most 100s in test matches","Most 100s in one-day international cricket","Most runs in one-day international cricket","Highest runs in a T20 match"],"a":3,"x":"Sachin Tendulkar holds the records for most Test centuries (51), most ODI centuries (49) and most ODI runs (18,426). He has no record for highest runs in a T20 match; he played only one T20 international.","e":"Virat Kohli has since passed his ODI centuries record.","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2025 S1"]}]},{"id":"misc","n":"Awards, Firsts, Days and Organisations","qs":[{"id":"q0079","q":"The Indian Classical languages Malayalam and Kannada are majorly spoken in the Indian states of __________ and Karnataka, respectively.","o":["Goa","Andhra Pradesh","Telangana","Kerala"],"a":3,"x":"Malayalam is the main language of Kerala and Kannada of Karnataka. Both are recognised classical languages (Kannada 2008, Malayalam 2013).","e":"Telugu was recognised along with Kannada in 2008.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0094","q":"In which year was Sanskrit declared a classical language of India?","o":["1976","1950","1999","2005"],"a":3,"x":"Sanskrit was declared a classical language in 2005, the second after Tamil (2004).","e":"Order: Tamil 2004, Sanskrit 2005, Telugu and Kannada 2008, Malayalam 2013, Odia 2014; Marathi, Pali, Prakrit, Assamese and Bengali in 2024.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0190","q":"Which of the following is true about the National Independence Day celebrations in India?","o":["It marks the date of adoption of the Indian Constitution.","It is celebrated on 15 August to mark the signing of the Treaty of Versailles.","It celebrates India's victory in the 1965 war with Pakistan.","The Prime Minister unfurls the national flag at Red Fort every year."],"a":3,"x":"On Independence Day (15 August) the Prime Minister hoists the national flag at the Red Fort, Delhi, and addresses the nation.","e":"On Republic Day the President unfurls the flag at Kartavya Path.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0238","q":"What is one of the key criterion for a language to be declared a Classical Language?","o":["It must not have a history of more than 500 years.","It must have a body of ancient literature and texts.","It must be widely spoken in multiple regions.","It must be taught in all educational institutions."],"a":1,"x":"A classical language must have ancient texts or recorded history over 1,500–2,000 years, a body of valued ancient literature, and a literary tradition of its own.","e":"India now has 11 classical languages.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0268","q":"In which of the following states of India, the World War II Cemetery, which was found to have around 1000 graves at the time of its discovery in 1997, is located?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Gujarat","Goa","Arunachal Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"The Jairampur (Pangsau Pass) World War II cemetery in Changlang district, Arunachal Pradesh, was found in 1997 with about 1,000 graves of Allied soldiers from the Stilwell Road work.","e":"The Stilwell (Ledo) Road linked Ledo in Assam to China through Myanmar.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0343","q":"In which script is the language Hindi, an offshoot of Sanskrit, traditionally written?","o":["Sinhala","Gurumukhi","Nandinagari","Devanagari"],"a":3,"x":"Hindi is written in the Devanagari script, as are Sanskrit, Marathi and Nepali.","e":"Gurmukhi is used for Punjabi; Nandinagari is an old southern variant used for Sanskrit.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0421","q":"Where is the Central Institute of Indian Languages located?","o":["Bengaluru","New Delhi","Mumbai","Mysuru"],"a":3,"x":"The Central Institute of Indian Languages (CIIL) is in Mysuru, Karnataka; it was set up in 1969.","e":"Mysuru also hosts the All India Institute of Speech and Hearing.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0443","q":"Where is the Central Institute of Indian Languages located?","o":["Hyderabad","Mysuru","Thiruvananthapuram","Bengaluru"],"a":1,"x":"The Central Institute of Indian Languages (CIIL) is in Mysuru, Karnataka, under the Ministry of Education.","e":"It was set up in 1969.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0448","q":"Which of the following is a criterion for a language to be declared as classical in India, as per the revised criteria shared by the Government of India in 2024?","o":["The language must have a recorded history spanning at least 500 years.","The language must be spoken by at least 10 million people.","The language should have a body of ancient literature considered a valuable heritage by generations of speakers.","The language should have originated from Sanskrit."],"a":2,"x":"Under the revised criteria (2024), a classical language needs a recorded history of 1,500–2,000 years, ancient literature valued as heritage, and knowledge texts (prose, inscriptions).","e":"Marathi, Pali, Prakrit, Assamese and Bengali were added in October 2024.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0449","q":"The Human Development Report is released by UNDP. The headquarter of UNDP is located at which of the following places?","o":["New York, USA","Paris, France","Geneva, Switzerland","Basel, Switzerland"],"a":0,"x":"UNDP is headquartered in New York and publishes the Human Development Report every year.","e":"UNESCO: Paris. WHO: Geneva. World Bank: Washington DC.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0480","q":"In which year was the Central Institute of Indian Languages at Mysore set up, to help in evolving and implementing the language policy of the Government of India?","o":["1971","1965","1969","1967"],"a":2,"x":"The Central Institute of Indian Languages was set up at Mysore on 17 July 1969 to help develop and implement India's language policy.","e":"It works under the Ministry of Education.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1591","q":"The Lalit Kala Akademi was inaugurated in New Delhi in the year _____.","o":["1953","1954","1952","1951"],"a":1,"x":"The Lalit Kala Akademi (National Academy of Art) was inaugurated in New Delhi in 1954.","e":"Sangeet Natak Akademi and Sahitya Akademi were also set up in the early 1950s.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1612","q":"Which Prime Minister declared India a full-fledged nuclear weapon state?","o":["Narendra Modi","Indira Gandhi","Atal Bihari Vajpayee","Manmohan Singh"],"a":2,"x":"After the Pokhran-II tests in May 1998, Prime Minister Atal Bihari Vajpayee declared India a nuclear weapon state.","e":"The tests were code-named Operation Shakti; 11 May is National Technology Day.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q0504","q":"Chandrayaan-1 was India's first lunar probe. It was launched under the chairmanship of __________.","o":["Krishnaswamy Kasturirangan","K. Radhakrishnan","A. S. Kiran Kumar","G. Madhavan Nair"],"a":3,"x":"Chandrayaan-1 was launched on 22 October 2008 from Sriharikota when G. Madhavan Nair was ISRO chairman; its instruments helped confirm water on the Moon.","e":"Chandrayaan-3 landed near the lunar south pole on 23 August 2023, now National Space Day.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0506","q":"Malala Yousafzai received the Nobel Prize for Peace at the age of _______.","o":["20","17","22","19"],"a":1,"x":"Malala Yousafzai won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2014 at 17, the youngest Nobel laureate; she shared it with India's Kailash Satyarthi.","e":"Both campaigned for children's rights and education.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0507","q":"Subrahmanyam Chandrasekhar received the Nobel Prize in 1983 for his contribution in the field of __________.","o":["physics","literature","chemistry","peace"],"a":0,"x":"Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar won the 1983 Nobel Prize in Physics for work on the structure and evolution of stars; the Chandrasekhar limit is named after him.","e":"C. V. Raman, his uncle, won Physics in 1930 for the Raman effect.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0544","q":"Which among the following awards are famously known as 'Oscars'?","o":["Academy Awards","Grammy Awards","Emmy Awards","Golden Globe Awards"],"a":0,"x":"The Oscars are the Academy Awards, given since 1929 by the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences, USA.","e":"Grammy: music. Emmy: television.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0545","q":"Who among the following was known as the \"Young Turk\"?","o":["Jawaharlal Nehru","Ashutosh Mukherji","Chandra Shekhar","Bal Gangadhar Tilak"],"a":2,"x":"Chandra Shekhar, later Prime Minister (1990–91), was called the 'Young Turk' for leading a group of young radical Congress leaders in the late 1960s and 1970s.","e":"He was the first PM who had never held a minister's post before.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0584","q":"Who among the following didn't receive the Bharat Ratna award in 2019?","o":["Bhupen Hazarika","Nanaji Deshmukh","Pranab Mukherjee","Virat Kohli"],"a":3,"x":"Bharat Ratna 2019 went to Pranab Mukherjee, Nanaji Deshmukh (posthumous) and Bhupen Hazarika (posthumous). Virat Kohli was not a recipient.","e":"Sachin Tendulkar (2014) is the only sportsperson to get the Bharat Ratna.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0586","q":"Where is the headquarters of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations located?","o":["China","Indonesia","Nepal","Vietnam"],"a":1,"x":"ASEAN's secretariat is in Jakarta, Indonesia; ASEAN was founded in 1967 by the Bangkok Declaration.","e":"Timor-Leste became its 11th member in 2025.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0588","q":"Every year, the state of Odisha celebrates Utkal Divas on __________.","o":["1st April","31st march","2nd April","29th March"],"a":0,"x":"Odisha celebrates Utkal Divas (Odisha Day) on 1 April, marking its formation as a separate province in 1936.","e":"It was the first province formed on a linguistic basis in British India.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0624","q":"Which among the following countries is not a member of the Group of Eight (G8)?","o":["United States","Japan","United Kingdom","India"],"a":3,"x":"India is not a member of the G8 (USA, UK, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Canada, Russia); it became the G7 after Russia's suspension in 2014.","e":"India is a member of the G20 and hosted its 2023 summit.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0626","q":"Kalinga Prize is an award given by UNESCO for the contribution in the field of ____________.","o":["literature","science","peace","architecture"],"a":1,"x":"UNESCO's Kalinga Prize (from 1952, funded by Biju Patnaik) honours popularisation of science.","e":"The first winner was Louis de Broglie.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0627","q":"Which among the following was India's first experimental satellite launch vehicle?","o":["SLV-4","SLV-1","SLV-3","SLV-2"],"a":2,"x":"SLV-3 was India's first experimental satellite launch vehicle; it put the Rohini satellite into orbit in July 1980 under project director Dr A. P. J. Abdul Kalam.","e":"Later vehicles: ASLV, PSLV, GSLV, LVM3.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0664","q":"Wangari Maathai is the first African woman to be awarded the Nobel Prize for her contribution in the field of __________.","o":["chemistry","physics","literature","peace"],"a":3,"x":"Wangari Maathai of Kenya won the 2004 Nobel Peace Prize, the first African woman Nobel laureate, for sustainable development and her Green Belt Movement.","e":"Her movement planted over 30 million trees.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0666","q":"Who among the following gave the slogan \"Jai Jawan Jai Kisan\"?","o":["Subhash Chandra Bose","Jawaharlal Nehru","Lal Bahadur Shastri","Mahatma Gandhi"],"a":2,"x":"Lal Bahadur Shastri gave the slogan 'Jai Jawan Jai Kisan' in 1965, during the war with Pakistan and food shortages.","e":"Atal Bihari Vajpayee added 'Jai Vigyan' in 1998.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0667","q":"Which among the following was replaced by the World Trade Organisation?","o":["General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade","International Monetary Fund","Trade-Related Investment Measures","Trade-Related Intellectual Property Rights"],"a":0,"x":"The World Trade Organization (1 January 1995, Geneva) replaced the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT, 1947), following the Uruguay Round.","e":"India is a founder member of both GATT and the WTO.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0704","q":"For which of the following films did Bhanu Athaiya receive an oscar for the best costume designer?","o":["Swades","Lagaan: Once Upon a Time in India","Gandhi","Slumdog Millionaire"],"a":2,"x":"Bhanu Athaiya won the Oscar for Best Costume Design for 'Gandhi' (1982), the first Indian to win an Academy Award.","e":"A. R. Rahman and Resul Pookutty won for 'Slumdog Millionaire' (2009).","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0706","q":"Who among the following was the first chairman of the ISRO?","o":["Satish Dhawan","Vikram Sarabhai","Shailesh Nayak","A. S. Kiran Kumar"],"a":1,"x":"Dr Vikram Sarabhai, father of India's space programme, was ISRO's first chairman (1969).","e":"ISRO grew out of INCOSPAR (1962).","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0707","q":"Where was the first session of United Nations held?","o":["London","Paris","Geneva","New York"],"a":0,"x":"The first session of the UN General Assembly opened in London (Methodist Central Hall) on 10 January 1946.","e":"The UN Charter was signed at San Francisco in June 1945; UN Day is 24 October.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0744","q":"Amongst the following, who created the \"You Said It\" comic strip featured in the The Times of India?","o":["Satish Acharya","R.K. Laxman","Sudhir Tailang","Abu Abraham"],"a":1,"x":"R. K. Laxman created the 'You Said It' cartoon strip with 'The Common Man' in The Times of India from 1951.","e":"His brother R. K. Narayan wrote 'Malgudi Days'.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0745","q":"Amongst the following, name India's highest military honour for distinguished valour during wartime.","o":["Param Vir Chakra","Ashok Chakra","Kirti Chakra","Maha Vir Chakra"],"a":0,"x":"The Param Vir Chakra is India's highest military award for the highest gallantry in the presence of the enemy.","e":"The Ashoka Chakra is the highest peacetime gallantry award.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0747","q":"What was the code name for India's first nuclear test conducted at Pokhran in 1974?","o":["Operation Shakti","Peaceful Buddha","Operation Bandar","Smiling Buddha"],"a":3,"x":"India's first nuclear test at Pokhran on 18 May 1974 was code-named 'Smiling Buddha'.","e":"The 1998 tests were 'Operation Shakti'.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1760","q":"Tenzing Norgay Award is associated with which field?","o":["Adventure","Arts","Music","Journalism"],"a":0,"x":"The Tenzing Norgay National Adventure Award is India's highest award for adventure sports on land, sea and air.","e":"Named after Tenzing Norgay, who climbed Everest with Hillary in 1953.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1763","q":"Which of the following statement is true about Defence Research and Development Organisation? I. It’s headquarter is located in Mumbai. II. It carries a vision to empower India with cutting-edge defence technologies.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"DRDO is headquartered in New Delhi, not Mumbai, so statement I is wrong; its vision is to empower India with cutting-edge defence technologies.","e":"DRDO was formed in 1958.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1768","q":"On which technology does FASTag for toll collection works?","o":["Wireless Frequency Identification","Ultrasound","Radio Frequency Identification","Artificial Intelligence"],"a":2,"x":"FASTag uses Radio Frequency Identification (RFID): a tag on the windscreen is read by radio waves at the toll plaza, and the toll is deducted automatically.","e":"NHAI runs FASTag; it became mandatory in February 2021.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1803","q":"Who is known as the Missile Man of India?","o":["A P J Abdul Kalam","Homi J. Bhabha","Vikram Sarabhai","Ratan Kumar Sinha"],"a":0,"x":"A.P.J. Abdul Kalam is called the Missile Man of India for leading the Agni and Prithvi missile programmes; he was President from 2002 to 2007.","e":"Homi Bhabha: nuclear programme; Vikram Sarabhai: space programme.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1804","q":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy award is associated with which field?","o":["Journalism","Literature","Environment Protection","Science"],"a":0,"x":"The Raja Ram Mohan Roy Award is given by the Press Council of India on National Press Day (16 November) for outstanding contribution to journalism.","e":"Ram Mohan Roy published 'Sambad Kaumudi' and 'Mirat-ul-Akhbar'.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1806","q":"Where are the head quarters of World Bank located?","o":["Washington DC","Vienna","Budapest","Paris"],"a":0,"x":"The World Bank and the IMF are both headquartered in Washington, D.C., USA; both came from the Bretton Woods Conference (1944).","e":"WTO: Geneva; UNESCO: Paris.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1844","q":"International Monetary Fund has its headquarters in ______.","o":["London","Washington D.C.","Rome","Geneva"],"a":1,"x":"The International Monetary Fund is headquartered in Washington, D.C., like the World Bank.","e":"IMF and World Bank = Bretton Woods twins (1944).","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1956","q":"Which organization releases 'Ease of Doing Business' report?","o":["Asian Development Bank","United Nation Development Programme","World Bank","International Monetary Fund"],"a":2,"x":"The World Bank published the 'Ease of Doing Business' report; it stopped the series in 2021 after data irregularities.","e":"The World Bank's 'Business Ready' (B-READY) report replaced it.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1999","q":"The North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) headquarters is located in ________.","o":["Italy","Belgium","Germany","France"],"a":1,"x":"NATO (formed 1949) is headquartered in Brussels, Belgium.","e":"EU institutions are also in Brussels.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2086","q":"Which of the following reports is not published by World Bank?","o":["Human Development Report","Ease of Doing Business","World Development Report","Global Economic Prospects"],"a":0,"x":"The Human Development Report is published by UNDP, not the World Bank. The World Development Report, Global Economic Prospects and (until 2021) Ease of Doing Business are World Bank reports.","e":"HDR = UNDP since 1990.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2087","q":"Which of the following has achieved the recognition of the highest literary award of India?","o":["Saraswati Samman","Vyas Samman","Jnanpith Award","Sahitya Akademi Award"],"a":2,"x":"The Jnanpith Award (since 1965) is India's highest literary award; G. Sankara Kurup was the first winner.","e":"Saraswati Samman and Vyas Samman are given by the K.K. Birla Foundation.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2088","q":"Who is known as the ‘Father of the Indian Nuclear Programme’?","o":["M.S. Swaminathan","Homi Jehangir Bhabha","Vikram Sarabhai","C.N.R. Rao"],"a":1,"x":"Homi Jehangir Bhabha is called the father of India's nuclear programme; he founded TIFR (1945) and the Atomic Energy Establishment, now BARC.","e":"Vikram Sarabhai: father of the Indian space programme.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2413","q":"In which year was the International Monetary Fund established?","o":["1942","1943","1944","1946"],"a":2,"x":"The IMF was created at the Bretton Woods Conference in July 1944 (in force from December 1945) and is headquartered in Washington, D.C.","e":"IMF and World Bank = Bretton Woods twins.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2455","q":"Where is the headquarters of the World Meteorological Organization situated?","o":["Paris","London","New York","Geneva"],"a":3,"x":"The World Meteorological Organization (a UN agency, 1950) is headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland.","e":"World Meteorological Day: 23 March.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2457","q":"To whom is Shaurya Chakra awarded?","o":["Military personnels","Civilians","Athletes","Both military personnels and civilians"],"a":3,"x":"The Shaurya Chakra, a peacetime gallantry award, can be given to both military personnel and civilians.","e":"Peacetime order: Ashoka Chakra → Kirti Chakra → Shaurya Chakra.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2498","q":"Who is the first physician in India to create a test-tube baby?","o":["Dr. Devi Sethi","Dr. Subhash Mukherjee","Dr. Rajeev Ghosh","Dr. Sayan Majumder"],"a":1,"x":"Dr Subhash Mukhopadhyay of Kolkata created India's first test-tube baby, Durga (Kanupriya Agarwal), born in October 1978.","e":"It came only 67 days after the world's first, Louise Brown.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2500","q":"Where is the headquarters of IORA (Indian Ocean Rim Association) situated?","o":["Ebene","Abu Dhabi","Port Louis","Male"],"a":0,"x":"The Indian Ocean Rim Association (IORA, 1997) has its secretariat at Ebene, Mauritius.","e":"It has 23 member states.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2501","q":"Which of the following dates has been declared as International Anti-Corruption Day by the United Nations?","o":["9th December","15th January","24th August","3rd April"],"a":0,"x":"The UN observes International Anti-Corruption Day on 9 December, marking the UN Convention against Corruption (2003).","e":"Human Rights Day is the next day, 10 December.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2541","q":"In which year did the World Health Organization begin its operations?","o":["1948","1949","1950","1951"],"a":0,"x":"The World Health Organization began work on 7 April 1948, now World Health Day; it is headquartered in Geneva.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2681","q":"The Saraswati Samman is given every year for the outstanding work in the field of _______.","o":["Science","Journalism","Dance","Literature"],"a":3,"x":"The Saraswati Samman, given by the K.K. Birla Foundation since 1991, honours outstanding literary work in an Indian language.","e":"First winner: Harivansh Rai Bachchan.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1314","q":"Who out of the following had served as the first Indian Commander-in-Chief of the Indian Army?","o":["General Sam Manekshaw","General Arjan Singh","General K.M. Cariappa","General Maharaj Rajendra Sinhji"],"a":2,"x":"General K. M. Cariappa became the first Indian Commander-in-Chief of the Indian Army on 15 January 1949, marked as Army Day.","e":"Cariappa and Sam Manekshaw are the only two Field Marshals of the Indian Army.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1318","q":"On which of the following days is the Padma Awards announced?","o":["On Shaheed Diwas","On the eve of the Republic Day","On the Independence Day","On the eve of the Independence Day"],"a":1,"x":"The Padma Awards (Padma Vibhushan, Padma Bhushan, Padma Shri) are announced on the eve of Republic Day each year.","e":"They were instituted in 1954.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1319","q":"Which of the following days was observed as the Women's Day in 2020?","o":["March 8th","March 5th","March 6th","March 7th"],"a":0,"x":"International Women's Day is observed on 8 March every year.","e":"National Women's Day in India is 13 February, Sarojini Naidu's birthday.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1356","q":"The Pritzker Prize is awarded for the achievement in the field of _______________.","o":["literature","music","mathematics","architecture"],"a":3,"x":"The Pritzker Architecture Prize is often called architecture's Nobel. B. V. Doshi (2018) was the first Indian winner.","e":"Balkrishna Doshi designed IIM Bangalore.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0953","q":"National Education Day, observed on 11 November 2025, is celebrated in honour of the birth anniversary of __________.","o":["Rabindranath Tagore","Dr. S. Radhakrishnan","Maulana Abul Kalam Azad","A. P. J. Abdul Kalam"],"a":2,"x":"National Education Day (11 November) marks the birth anniversary of Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, India's first Education Minister.","e":"Teachers' Day (5 September) marks S. Radhakrishnan's birthday.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q0959","q":"ISRO is planning to launch which of the following tentatively named missions to explore Venus?","o":["Shukrayaan-1","Mangalyaan-2","Chandrayaan-3","Aditya L-1"],"a":0,"x":"Shukrayaan-1 is ISRO's planned orbiter mission to study Venus; the Venus Orbiter Mission was approved in 2024.","e":"Chandrayaan = Moon, Mangalyaan = Mars, Aditya-L1 = Sun, Shukrayaan = Venus.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1038","q":"The Automatic Train Protection (ATP) system, KAVACH, was indigenously developed by which of the following agencies to help Indian Railways achieve zero accidents?","o":["Research Designs and Standards Organisation (RDSO)","Bharat Electronics Limited (BEL)","Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited (BHEL)","Integral Coach Factory (ICF)"],"a":0,"x":"Kavach, India's automatic train protection system, was developed by the Research Designs and Standards Organisation (RDSO), Lucknow, with Indian industry.","e":"It applies brakes automatically if the driver misses a signal.","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1082","q":"'Ustad Bismillah Khan Yuva Puraskar' is given to Indian artists up to __________ of age.","o":["30 years","40 years","45 years","35 years"],"a":1,"x":"The Ustad Bismillah Khan Yuva Puraskar, given by the Sangeet Natak Akademi since 2006, honours young artists aged up to 40 in music, dance and drama.","e":"Named after the shehnai maestro, a Bharat Ratna (2001).","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1238","q":"Which of the following satellites was launched for telecommunications and meteorological observation?","o":["IRS","GSAT","INSAT","Chandrayaan"],"a":2,"x":"INSAT (Indian National Satellite System, from 1983) is a series of geostationary satellites for telecommunications, broadcasting and weather observation.","e":"IRS = remote sensing; GSAT = mainly communication.","src":["PRT 3 Apr 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1263","q":"The World Energy Outlook 2025 was released by which organisation?","o":["International Monetary Fund","International Energy Agency","Organization Petroleum Energy Agency","World Meteorological Organization of Energy"],"a":1,"x":"The World Energy Outlook is published every year by the International Energy Agency (IEA), headquartered in Paris.","e":"OPEC, based in Vienna, publishes the World Oil Outlook.","src":["PRT 10 Apr 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1386","q":"Where is the India’s first Dark Night Sky Reserve being established?","o":["Ladakh","Daman and Diu","Lakshadweep","Dadra and Nagar Haveli"],"a":0,"x":"India's first Dark Sky Reserve is at Hanle in Ladakh, set up in 2022 around the Indian Astronomical Observatory.","e":"Hanle's observatory is among the world's highest.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1396","q":"What name was given to the operation for liberation of Goa, in 1961, to make it a part of the Union of India?","o":["Operation Vijay","Operation Margao","Operation Polo","Operation Searchlight"],"a":0,"x":"Operation Vijay (December 1961) liberated Goa, Daman and Diu from Portuguese rule; 19 December is Goa Liberation Day.","e":"Operation Polo (1948) integrated Hyderabad.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1415","q":"Which of the following man-made objects was the first one to cross the heliosphere of our solar system?","o":["Sputnik-1","New Horizons","Messenger-1","Voyager-1"],"a":3,"x":"Voyager 1 (launched 1977) became the first human-made object to cross the heliosphere into interstellar space, in August 2012.","e":"Voyager 2 crossed in 2018.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1455","q":"What is the angle of inclination of the International Space Station’s orbit compared to the equator?","o":["66.8 degrees","0 degrees","51.6 degrees","22.5 degrees"],"a":2,"x":"The International Space Station orbits at an inclination of about 51.6° to the equator, at around 400 km altitude.","e":"It circles Earth about every 90 minutes.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1484","q":"On which date is the International Day of Non-Violence observed?","o":["2 November","2 December","2 January","2 October"],"a":3,"x":"The UN observes the International Day of Non-Violence on 2 October, Gandhi's birthday (adopted 2007).","e":"In India, 2 October is Gandhi Jayanti, a national holiday.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1488","q":"Good Governance Day (25 December) commemorates the birth anniversary of which former Prime Minister?","o":["Inder Kumar Gujral","Haradanahalli Doddegowda Deve Gowda","Atal Bihari Vajpayee","Vishwanath Pratap Singh"],"a":2,"x":"Good Governance Day is observed on 25 December, the birth anniversary of former Prime Minister Atal Bihari Vajpayee.","e":"Vajpayee received the Bharat Ratna in 2015.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1489","q":"In which year was Odia given Classical Language status?","o":["2011","2015","2012","2014"],"a":3,"x":"Odia was declared a classical language in 2014, the sixth after Tamil, Sanskrit, Telugu, Kannada and Malayalam.","e":"It was the first Indo-Aryan language after Sanskrit to get the status.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1492","q":"In which of the following states is Kannada used as the official language?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Tamil Nadu","Karnataka","Kerala"],"a":2,"x":"Kannada is the official language of Karnataka.","e":"Telugu: Andhra Pradesh and Telangana; Tamil: Tamil Nadu; Malayalam: Kerala.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1495","q":"Indian Air Force approved the production of Astra Mark-1(MK-1) missile. Which of the following types of missile is Astra Mark-1?","o":["Air to Air","Surface to Air","Surface to Surface","Air to Surface"],"a":0,"x":"Astra Mk-1 is an indigenous beyond-visual-range air-to-air missile developed by DRDO for the Indian Air Force.","e":"Akash is surface-to-air; BrahMos is a supersonic cruise missile.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1512","q":"In which of the following cities is the headquarters of the Central Institute of Hindi (Kendriya Hindi Sansthan) located?","o":["Agra","Jalandhar","Mumbai","Patna"],"a":0,"x":"The Kendriya Hindi Sansthan (Central Institute of Hindi) has its headquarters in Agra, Uttar Pradesh.","e":"It trains teachers of Hindi as a second or foreign language.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1555","q":"The Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) was formed in August ________.","o":["1969","1960","1976","1955"],"a":0,"x":"ISRO was formed on 15 August 1969, replacing INCOSPAR; its headquarters is in Bengaluru.","e":"India's first satellite, Aryabhata, was launched in 1975 on a Soviet rocket.","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1563","q":"The Kendriya Hindi Sansthan was established in the year _________ under the Ministry of Human Resources Development (Ministry of Education).","o":["1965","1954","1951","1961"],"a":3,"x":"The Kendriya Hindi Sansthan was set up in 1961 under the Ministry of Education (then HRD), with its headquarters at Agra.","e":"The Central Hindi Directorate was set up in 1960.","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0770","q":"Which language was the first to get classical language status in India, in 2004?","o":["Sanskrit","Kannada","Tamil","Telugu"],"a":2,"x":"Tamil was the first language declared classical in India, in 2004.","e":"Sanskrit followed in 2005.","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0799","q":"The Sandalwood film industry is associated with which language?","o":["Kannada","Telugu","Malayalam","Tamil"],"a":0,"x":"'Sandalwood' is the nickname of the Kannada film industry, based in Bengaluru.","e":"Tollywood: Telugu. Kollywood: Tamil. Mollywood: Malayalam.","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0841","q":"Which of the following is NOT a national festival of India?","o":["Gandhi Jayanti","Constitution Day","Republic Day","Independence Day"],"a":1,"x":"India's three national festivals are Republic Day (26 January), Independence Day (15 August) and Gandhi Jayanti (2 October). Constitution Day (26 November) is observed but is not a national festival.","e":"Constitution Day has been observed since 2015.","src":["PGT 14 Jul 2025"]},{"id":"q0842","q":"In which of the following cities of India is the headquarters of National Council for Promotion of Urdu Language located?","o":["Agra","Mumbai","New Delhi","Hyderabad"],"a":2,"x":"The National Council for Promotion of Urdu Language is headquartered in New Delhi, under the Ministry of Education.","e":"It started work in 1996.","src":["PGT 14 Jul 2025"]},{"id":"q0851","q":"In which of the following years did the National Council for Promotion of Urdu Language start its operations?","o":["1996","1997","1999","1998"],"a":0,"x":"The National Council for Promotion of Urdu Language began operating in 1996, in New Delhi.","e":"Urdu is one of the 22 languages in the Eighth Schedule.","src":["PGT 14 Jul 2025"]},{"id":"q0876","q":"On which date is National Broadcasting Day observed in India every year?","o":["15 August","22 July","1 August","23 July"],"a":3,"x":"National Broadcasting Day is observed on 23 July: on that day in 1927 the Indian Broadcasting Company began regular radio broadcasts from Bombay.","e":"The Indian State Broadcasting Service became All India Radio in 1936.","src":["PGT 24 Sep 2025"]}]}]});
